/* BlueForge training content – original questions written and independently checked for this app (Sept 2026).
   Baseline BS 7671:2018+A2:2022 / GN3. AM2E checklist from NET candidate guidance, in our own words. */
window.BF_QB = [{"id":"a001","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"What is the legal status of BS 7671:2018?","options":["A statutory instrument, enforced directly by the HSE with its own penalties","Guidance under the HSW Act 1974 that has the full force of law","A legal requirement for new-build dwellings only, under the Building Act","Non-statutory, but compliance is likely to satisfy relevant statutory duties such as the EAWR 1989"],"answer":3,"explain":"BS 7671 is not itself law. It is a code of practice that courts and enforcing bodies accept as evidence of good practice, and it is referred to by documents such as the Memorandum of Guidance on the EAWR and Approved Document P.","ref":"BS 7671 Introduction / Appendix 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a002","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"In which Part of BS 7671:2018 would you find the definitions of terms such as 'exposed-conductive-part' and 'skilled person'?","options":["Part 3","Part 1","Part 2","Part 5"],"answer":2,"explain":"Part 2 is Definitions. Part 1 covers scope, object and fundamental principles; Part 3 covers assessment of general characteristics; Part 5 covers selection and erection of equipment.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a003","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"Requirements for initial verification and periodic inspection and testing are contained in which Part of BS 7671?","options":["Part 6","Part 5","Part 7","Part 4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Part 6 is Inspection and testing (Chapter 64 initial verification, Chapter 65 periodic inspection and testing). Part 7 covers special installations or locations.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 6","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a004","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"In the regulation number 411.3.3, what does '41' identify?","options":["Appendix 41","Part 41","Section 41","Chapter 41"],"answer":3,"explain":"BS 7671 numbering runs Part–Chapter–Section–Regulation: '4' is Part 4, '41' is Chapter 41 (Protection against electric shock), '411' is Section 411 (ADS) and 411.3.3 is the individual regulation.","ref":"BS 7671 numbering convention","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a005","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"A regulation such as 522.6.202 or 421.1.201 has a final number in the 200 series. What does this indicate?","options":["It applies only to Part 7 special locations","It was introduced by Amendment 2:2022","It is a recommendation, not a requirement","It is a UK-specific (national) regulation"],"answer":3,"explain":"Regulations numbered 200 and upward in their final group are national (UK) additions that do not appear in the harmonised European/IEC documents on which BS 7671 is based.","ref":"BS 7671 numbering convention","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a006","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"Which of the following is excluded from the scope of BS 7671?","options":["Construction and demolition site installations","Electric vehicle charging installations","Solar photovoltaic power supply systems","Electrical equipment of aircraft"],"answer":3,"explain":"Regulation 110.2 excludes items such as equipment of aircraft, railway traction equipment, systems for distribution of electricity to the public and equipment on ships. Construction sites, PV and EV charging all have their own sections in Part 7.","ref":"Reg 110.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a007","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"Extra-low voltage (ELV) is defined as not exceeding:","options":["25 V a.c. or 60 V ripple-free d.c.","50 V a.c. or 120 V ripple-free d.c.","110 V a.c. or 150 V ripple-free d.c.","50 V a.c. or 50 V ripple-free d.c."],"answer":1,"explain":"Part 2 defines ELV as not exceeding 50 V a.c. or 120 V ripple-free d.c., whether between conductors or to earth. 25 V a.c./60 V d.c. are lower thresholds used elsewhere (e.g. where SELV basic protection may be omitted in dry conditions).","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a008","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"On a job you are connecting a Class I immersion heater. Its metal casing, which can be touched and is not normally live but could become live under fault conditions, is classed as:","options":["An extraneous-conductive-part","A protective conductor","A live part","An exposed-conductive-part"],"answer":3,"explain":"An exposed-conductive-part is a conductive part of equipment that can be touched, is not normally live, but can become live under fault conditions. It is part of the electrical equipment, which is what distinguishes it from an extraneous-conductive-part.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a009","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"A metallic water service pipe entering a building from the ground, not forming part of the electrical installation but able to introduce Earth potential, is classed as:","options":["An exposed-conductive-part","A functional earth","An extraneous-conductive-part","A circuit protective conductor"],"answer":2,"explain":"An extraneous-conductive-part is liable to introduce a potential, generally Earth potential, and does not form part of the electrical installation. Incoming metal services are the classic example and require main protective bonding.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a010","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"'Basic protection' is defined as protection against electric shock:","options":["Provided by an RCD of 30 mA or less","Under fault-free conditions","Under single-fault conditions","By automatic disconnection only"],"answer":1,"explain":"Basic protection (formerly direct contact protection) prevents contact with live parts in normal service, e.g. by insulation, barriers or enclosures. Protection under single-fault conditions is fault protection.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a011","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"The definition of 'arm's reach' extends vertically upwards from the surface on which a person stands to a height of:","options":["1.25 m","2.5 m","3.0 m","2.25 m"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arm's reach extends 2.5 m above the standing surface, 1.25 m horizontally and 0.75 m below. 2.25 m is the height limit of bathroom zone 1, a common distractor.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2 (arm's reach)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a012","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"A person adequately advised or supervised by electrically skilled persons so that they can perceive risks and avoid hazards is defined as:","options":["An instructed person (electrically)","A competent person","An ordinary person","A skilled person (electrically)"],"answer":0,"explain":"An instructed person is advised or supervised by skilled persons. A skilled person has the relevant education, training and practical skills themselves. An ordinary person is neither.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a013","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"You run a cable from the main consumer unit in a house to a separate consumer unit in a detached garage. In BS 7671 terms this cable forms:","options":["A ring final circuit","An unfused spur","A final circuit","A distribution circuit"],"answer":3,"explain":"A distribution circuit connects the origin (or an upstream board) to another distribution board or consumer unit. A final circuit connects directly to current-using equipment or socket-outlets. The distinction matters because distribution circuits have 5 s (TN) / 1 s (TT) disconnection times.","ref":"BS 7671 Part 2","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a014","topic":"Regs structure & definitions","q":"In the classification of external influences, the code category 'AD' relates to:","options":["Mechanical impact","Presence of foreign solid bodies","Ambient temperature","Presence of water"],"answer":3,"explain":"Appendix 5 codes: AA ambient temperature, AD presence of water, AE foreign solid bodies, AG impact. The first letter A denotes environment, B utilisation, C construction of buildings.","ref":"Appendix 5","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a015","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"For a 32 A final circuit in a TN system with U0 = 230 V, what is the maximum disconnection time required by Table 41.1?","options":["0.2 s","0.4 s","5 s","1 s"],"answer":1,"explain":"Table 41.1 gives 0.4 s for TN systems with 120 V < U0 ≤ 230 V. 0.2 s is the TT value; 5 s and 1 s apply to distribution circuits in TN and TT systems respectively.","ref":"Table 41.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a016","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"A 20 A socket-outlet final circuit forms part of a TT system at 230 V. What maximum disconnection time applies?","options":["0.07 s","0.4 s","1 s","0.2 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"For TT systems with 120 V < U0 ≤ 230 V, Table 41.1 requires 0.2 s. In practice this is achieved with an RCD because the earth electrode resistance is usually too high for an overcurrent device to operate fast enough.","ref":"Table 41.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a017","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"What is the maximum disconnection time permitted for a distribution circuit in a TN system?","options":["0.2 s","1 s","0.4 s","5 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"Distribution circuits, and final circuits outside the scope of Table 41.1, are allowed up to 5 s in a TN system (411.3.2.3). The TT equivalent is 1 s (411.3.2.4).","ref":"Reg 411.3.2.3","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a018","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"For a new socket-outlet circuit in a dwelling, 411.3.3 requires additional protection by an RCD with a rated residual operating current not exceeding 30 mA for socket-outlets rated up to:","options":["20 A","13 A","32 A","63 A"],"answer":2,"explain":"411.3.3 requires 30 mA RCD additional protection for socket-outlets with a rated current not exceeding 32 A (and for mobile equipment up to 32 A used outdoors).","ref":"Reg 411.3.3","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a019","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"A client asks you to omit RCD protection from a new 32 A radial socket circuit in their house, saying they'll sign a risk assessment. What is the correct position?","options":["Permitted if the circuit uses a Type C circuit-breaker instead","Permitted if the written risk assessment is attached to the EIC","Not permitted – the risk-assessment exception doesn't apply to dwellings","Permitted where all the sockets are mounted above 1.2 m"],"answer":2,"explain":"411.3.3 allows omission of RCD protection only where a documented risk assessment says it is unnecessary, and only for installations other than dwellings. In a house the 30 mA RCD is mandatory.","ref":"Reg 411.3.3","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a020","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Regulation 411.3.4 requires additional protection by a 30 mA RCD for which circuits in domestic (household) premises?","options":["Only circuits supplying bathroom luminaires","AC final circuits supplying luminaires","Distribution circuits to outbuildings","Only circuits run in metal conduit"],"answer":1,"explain":"411.3.4 was introduced in the 18th Edition and requires 30 mA RCD additional protection for all a.c. final circuits supplying luminaires within domestic premises.","ref":"Reg 411.3.4","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a021","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"An RCD used for additional protection must have a rated residual operating current not exceeding 30 mA and an operating time not exceeding:","options":["300 ms at 1 x IΔn","200 ms at 5 x IΔn","40 ms at 1 x IΔn","40 ms at 5 x IΔn"],"answer":3,"explain":"415.1.1 requires IΔn ≤ 30 mA and operation within 40 ms at a residual current of 5 x IΔn (150 mA for a 30 mA device). 300 ms at 1 x IΔn is the general non-delay RCD requirement, not the additional-protection criterion.","ref":"Reg 415.1.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a022","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Which statement about RCDs used for additional protection is correct?","options":["They may replace basic protection where the RCD is rated at 30 mA","They remove the need for a circuit protective conductor to each point","They are not a sole means of protection; ADS or another measure is still needed","They are only required where the maximum Zs values cannot be met"],"answer":2,"explain":"415.1.2 states that an RCD for additional protection is not a sole means of protection; one of the protective measures of Sections 411 to 414 (e.g. ADS) must still be applied.","ref":"Reg 415.1.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a023","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"What is the key difference between SELV and PELV?","options":["PELV requires a safety isolating transformer; SELV does not","SELV may only be used outdoors","SELV operates at up to 120 V a.c.; PELV at up to 50 V a.c.","PELV circuits are connected to earth; SELV circuits are not"],"answer":3,"explain":"Both are ELV systems supplied from a safety source such as a safety isolating transformer. In SELV the live parts are not connected to earth or to protective conductors of other systems; in PELV the circuit and/or exposed parts may be earthed.","ref":"Section 414","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a024","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Socket-outlets in a SELV system must:","options":["Be protected by a 30 mA RCD in addition to the safety source","Be BS 1363 13 A socket-outlets identified with a yellow label","Have no protective conductor contact and not accept other systems' plugs","Have a protective conductor contact connected to the main earthing terminal"],"answer":2,"explain":"414.4.4 requires SELV plugs and socket-outlets to be incompatible with those of other systems and SELV socket-outlets not to have a protective conductor contact, since SELV must not be connected to earth.","ref":"Reg 414.4.4","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a025","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"You are fitting a Class II luminaire with no earth terminal to an existing lighting point. What should you do with the circuit protective conductor at that point?","options":["Cut it back flush with the sheath, as a Class II luminaire has no use for it","Terminate it in a suitable terminal at the point, not connected to the luminaire","Connect it to the luminaire's metal fixing screw to earth the fitting","Fold it back and tape it to the neutral inside the ceiling rose"],"answer":1,"explain":"411.3.1.1 requires a cpc to be run to and terminated at each point in wiring and at each accessory. Terminating it allows a Class I luminaire to be fitted safely later; it must not be connected to the Class II equipment itself.","ref":"Reg 411.3.1.1","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a026","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Which protective measure is permitted only in installations controlled or supervised by skilled or instructed persons?","options":["Automatic disconnection of supply","Obstacles and placing out of reach","Double or reinforced insulation","SELV from a safety isolating transformer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Section 417 (obstacles and placing out of reach) gives only basic protection and is restricted to installations under the control or supervision of skilled or instructed persons. ADS, double insulation and SELV are generally permitted.","ref":"Section 417","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a027","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"The readily accessible top horizontal surface of an enclosure containing live parts must provide a degree of protection of at least:","options":["IPXXD or IP4X","IPXXB or IP2X","IP65","IPX4"],"answer":0,"explain":"Barriers and enclosures generally need at least IPXXB/IP2X (finger-safe), but readily accessible top horizontal surfaces need at least IPXXD/IP4X (1 mm wire) because small objects can be dropped onto them.","ref":"Regs 416.2.1 / 416.2.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a028","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"Within the protective measure 'automatic disconnection of supply', which of the following provides BASIC protection rather than fault protection?","options":["Basic insulation of live parts","Protective earthing","Automatic disconnection by a circuit-breaker","Main protective bonding"],"answer":0,"explain":"In ADS, basic protection is by insulation, barriers or enclosures. Fault protection is by protective earthing, protective equipotential bonding and automatic disconnection in case of a fault.","ref":"Section 411","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a029","topic":"Protection against shock","q":"In a TT system, the condition RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V must be met. What is the maximum earth electrode resistance (RA, including protective conductor) for a 100 mA RCD?","options":["167 Ω","1667 Ω","500 Ω","200 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"Rearranging: RA ≤ 50 / IΔn. RA ≤ 50 / 0.1 = 500 Ω. (1667 Ω would be for 30 mA and 167 Ω for 300 mA.)","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a030","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A domestic supply where the distributor provides the earth terminal from the combined neutral and earth (PEN) conductor of the supply cable is which type of system?","options":["TN-S","TN-C-S (PME)","TT","IT"],"answer":1,"explain":"In TN-C-S the neutral and protective functions are combined in a PEN conductor in the supply (PME) and separated at the origin of the installation. TN-S uses a separate earth, typically the supply cable sheath; TT uses the consumer's own earth electrode.","ref":"Part 3 / Part 2 (system types)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a031","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"In a TN-S system the means of earthing for the installation is typically provided by:","options":["The combined neutral and earth (PEN) conductor of the supply","A separate protective conductor, usually the supply cable sheath or armour","An earth electrode driven into the ground by the consumer","The incoming metallic water service pipe at its point of entry"],"answer":1,"explain":"TN-S keeps neutral and protective conductors separate throughout the system; the distributor's earth is often the lead sheath or armour of the service cable. Metal water pipes must never be used as the means of earthing.","ref":"Part 2 (system types)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a032","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"In the system designation 'TT', what does the second letter 'T' indicate?","options":["The source has one point connected directly to earth","Exposed-conductive-parts are earthed independently of the source earth","The source is isolated from earth or earthed via an impedance","Neutral and protective functions are combined in one conductor"],"answer":1,"explain":"First letter = relationship of the source to earth (T = directly earthed). Second letter = relationship of exposed-conductive-parts to earth (T = directly earthed independently of the source, N = connected to the earthed point of the source).","ref":"Part 2 (system types)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a033","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"What maximum external earth fault loop impedance (Ze) does a distributor typically quote for a TN-C-S (PME) supply?","options":["0.35 Ω","21 Ω","0.8 Ω","1.37 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"Typical distributor maxima are 0.35 Ω for TN-C-S, 0.8 Ω for TN-S and 21 Ω for TT (the latter excluding the consumer's electrode).","ref":"Distributor typical values (OSG/GN3)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a034","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A PME supply has a 25 mm² copper supply neutral. What is the minimum copper csa for the main protective bonding conductors?","options":["10 mm²","25 mm²","6 mm²","16 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 54.8: for a copper-equivalent supply neutral up to and including 35 mm², main bonding is 10 mm². 25 mm² ≤ 35 mm², so 10 mm² copper.","ref":"Table 54.8","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a035","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A commercial premises has a PME supply with a 70 mm² copper-equivalent neutral. What minimum copper main protective bonding conductor is required?","options":["16 mm²","25 mm²","10 mm²","35 mm²"],"answer":1,"explain":"Table 54.8 bands: ≤35 mm² → 10; >35–50 → 16; >50–95 → 25; >95–150 → 35; >150 → 50. 70 mm² falls in the >50–95 band, so 25 mm².","ref":"Table 54.8","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a036","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"For a TN-S installation (not PME) with a 16 mm² copper earthing conductor, what size copper main protective bonding conductor would normally be installed?","options":["10 mm²","4 mm²","6 mm²","16 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"544.1.1: main bonding must be at least half the csa required for the earthing conductor, minimum 6 mm². 16 / 2 = 8 mm²; the next standard size up is 10 mm².","ref":"Reg 544.1.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a037","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Using Table 54.7 (protective conductor of the same material as the line conductor), what is the minimum cpc size for a 25 mm² line conductor?","options":["10 mm²","12.5 mm²","25 mm²","16 mm²"],"answer":3,"explain":"Table 54.7: S ≤ 16 → same as S; 16 < S ≤ 35 → 16 mm²; S > 35 → S/2. 25 mm² is in the middle band, so 16 mm².","ref":"Table 54.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a038","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A separate protective conductor that does not form part of a cable or enclosure and is NOT mechanically protected must be at least:","options":["4 mm² copper","6 mm² copper","2.5 mm² copper","1.5 mm² copper"],"answer":0,"explain":"543.1.1: a protective conductor not forming part of a cable or cable enclosure must be at least 2.5 mm² copper if mechanically protected, or 4 mm² copper if not.","ref":"Reg 543.1.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a039","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"A supplementary bonding conductor connecting two extraneous-conductive-parts, without mechanical protection, must be at least:","options":["2.5 mm² copper","4 mm² copper","6 mm² copper","10 mm² copper"],"answer":1,"explain":"544.2.3: a supplementary bonding conductor between two extraneous-conductive-parts must be at least 2.5 mm² copper with mechanical protection or 4 mm² copper without.","ref":"Reg 544.2.3","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a040","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Where supplementary bonding is used in a circuit protected by a 30 mA RCD, the resistance R between simultaneously accessible parts must not exceed 50 V / IΔn. What is the maximum R?","options":["167 Ω","500 Ω","1667 Ω","50 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"R ≤ 50 / IΔn = 50 / 0.03 = 1666.7 Ω, i.e. 1667 Ω.","ref":"Reg 415.2.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a041","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Where a gas meter is inside the premises, the main protective bonding connection to the gas installation pipe should, where practicable, be made:","options":["At any convenient point on the gas pipework inside the building","At the boiler connection, where the pipework is usually most accessible to test","On the supply side of the meter, just before the emergency control valve","Consumer's side, before any branch, within 600 mm of the meter outlet union"],"answer":3,"explain":"544.1.2 requires the connection on the consumer's hard metal pipework before any branch, where practicable within 600 mm of the meter outlet union, or at the point of entry if the meter is external. Bonding on the supply side of the meter is not permitted.","ref":"Reg 544.1.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a042","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"What wording must be on the permanent label fixed at the connection of a main protective bonding conductor to a gas pipe?","options":["Safety Electrical Connection – Do Not Remove","Warning – Protective Conductor – Isolate Before Removing","Danger – Earth Bonding – Keep Clear","Earth Connection – Test Annually"],"answer":0,"explain":"514.13.1 requires a label 'Safety Electrical Connection – Do Not Remove' at the connection of every earthing conductor to an earth electrode and every bonding conductor to an extraneous-conductive-part.","ref":"Reg 514.13.1","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a043","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"The joint provided for disconnecting the earthing conductor (e.g. to measure Ze or RA) must be:","options":["Fitted with a finger-operated quick-release clamp","Located outside the building for access by the DNO","Permanently sealed so that it cannot be disconnected","Accessible, and removable only by means of a tool"],"answer":3,"explain":"542.4.2 requires a means of disconnecting the earthing conductor at an accessible position, mechanically strong, and able to be disconnected only with a tool, so testing can be carried out but casual removal is prevented.","ref":"Reg 542.4.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a044","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Why are PME earthing terminals restricted for supplies to locations such as caravan pitches and outdoor EV charge points?","options":["PME supplies cannot provide a low enough Ze to achieve 0.4 s disconnection","PME earthing prevents surge protective devices from operating correctly","PME supplies are incompatible with the use of 30 mA RCDs on final circuits","An open PEN could raise connected metalwork to a dangerous voltage relative to true earth"],"answer":3,"explain":"With an open PEN, load current returns via the protective conductors, so connected metalwork can approach line voltage. Outdoors, people standing on true earth (often wet, possibly barefoot) are at greater risk, so Part 7 and ESQCR restrict PME use.","ref":"Part 7 / ESQCR (PME)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a045","topic":"Earthing & bonding","q":"Which of the following would require main protective bonding in a typical dwelling?","options":["An aluminium window frame fixed in masonry","A stainless steel sink supplied by plastic pipework only","A metallic gas installation pipe entering the building","A metal door handle"],"answer":2,"explain":"411.3.1.2 requires bonding of extraneous-conductive-parts such as metallic water, gas and oil services, central heating and structural steel. Isolated metalwork that cannot introduce a potential is not an extraneous-conductive-part.","ref":"Reg 411.3.1.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a046","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"Which of the following is NOT permitted as a device for isolation?","options":["A double-pole switch-disconnector to BS EN 60947-3","A fuse carrier that can be withdrawn from its base","A semiconductor device such as an electronic dimmer","A linked circuit-breaker suitable for isolation"],"answer":2,"explain":"Semiconductor (solid-state) devices must not be used as isolating devices because they do not provide a visible or reliable contact gap. They may be used for functional switching.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a047","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"An off-load isolator for a motor is mounted out of sight of the motor. What must be provided?","options":["A 30 mA RCD mounted immediately beside the isolator","A means of securing it in the off position, e.g. a padlock","An indicator lamp to show when the motor is running","A second identical isolator installed in parallel with it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Where an isolator is remote from the equipment, or could be operated inadvertently, provision must be made to secure it in the open position (e.g. padlock) to prevent unintentional re-energising.","ref":"Section 537","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a048","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"For a single-phase installation intended for use by ordinary persons (e.g. a house), the main switch must:","options":["Be an RCD of 100 mA","Interrupt the line conductor only","Interrupt the line, neutral and protective conductors","Interrupt both the line and neutral conductors"],"answer":3,"explain":"BS 7671 requires the main linked switch for a household-type single-phase installation to be double-pole so that both live conductors are disconnected. The protective conductor must never be switched.","ref":"Chapter 46","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a049","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"In which earthing system must the neutral conductor always be isolated along with the line conductors?","options":["TN-C-S","TT","It never needs isolating","TN-S"],"answer":1,"explain":"In TN systems the neutral may in some cases be regarded as reliably at earth potential and need not be isolated. In a TT system the neutral cannot be assumed to be at earth potential, so all live conductors including the neutral must be isolated.","ref":"Section 461","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a050","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"A device for emergency switching should be:","options":["Readily accessible and cut off the supply directly with a single action","A plug and socket-outlet located close to the machine","Located behind a locked door to prevent misuse by staff","A functional switch that operates a control relay after a delay"],"answer":0,"explain":"Emergency switching must act quickly and directly on the supply with a single action, be readily accessible and clearly identified. A plug and socket-outlet must not be selected as a means of emergency switching.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a051","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"Where a push-button is used for emergency switching, it should normally be coloured:","options":["Black, on a grey background with a white legend","Green, on a contrasting white background","Red, on a contrasting (usually yellow) background","Yellow, on a contrasting red background"],"answer":2,"explain":"Emergency switching devices, including push-buttons, should be red with a contrasting background, typically yellow, so they are instantly identifiable.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a052","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"What is the purpose of functional switching?","options":["To remove an unexpected danger as quickly as possible","To make equipment dead so electrical work can be done safely","To control equipment on/off (or vary it) in normal use","To protect the circuit wiring against overcurrent"],"answer":2,"explain":"Functional switching controls normal operation (e.g. a light switch or dimmer). Isolation makes equipment safe for electrical work; emergency switching removes unexpected danger.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a053","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"A switch provided for mechanical maintenance of a machine must be designed or installed so that:","options":["It cannot be inadvertently reclosed while work is in progress","It interrupts only the neutral conductor of the motor supply","It is a semiconductor switch so that it operates very quickly","It automatically recloses after a set time delay has elapsed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Switching off for mechanical maintenance must prevent the machine restarting during non-electrical work, e.g. by locking or placing the switch under the control of the person doing the work.","ref":"Section 537","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a054","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"Where unexpected restarting of a motor after a supply failure could cause danger, the motor control must:","options":["Prevent automatic restarting when the supply is restored","Restart the motor automatically as quickly as possible","Include a time delay of at least 5 s before restarting","Use a Type D circuit-breaker"],"answer":0,"explain":"Section 552 requires motors, where unexpected restarting could cause danger, to have means (e.g. a contactor with no-volt release) to prevent automatic restarting after a drop or loss of voltage.","ref":"Section 552","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a055","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"Single-pole switching devices (e.g. a one-way light switch) must be connected in:","options":["The line conductor only","Either line or neutral","The protective conductor","The neutral conductor only"],"answer":0,"explain":"A single-pole switch in the neutral would leave the equipment live when switched off, creating danger during lamp changing or maintenance. Single-pole switching must be in the line conductor.","ref":"Chapter 53","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a056","topic":"Isolation & switching","q":"The position indicator of a device for isolation must:","options":["Show OFF only when every pole's contacts have fully opened","Show a green lamp whenever the load is energised","Display the load current flowing through the device","Be visible only when the enclosure cover is removed"],"answer":0,"explain":"An isolating device must reliably show its contact position; the OFF indication must only appear when every pole has actually opened to the required isolating distance.","ref":"Section 537","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a057","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"A cable concealed in a wall at a depth of less than 50 mm may run horizontally within a prescribed zone measured from the top of the wall or partition. How wide is that zone?","options":["200 mm","100 mm","150 mm","300 mm"],"answer":2,"explain":"522.6.202 allows cables within 150 mm of the top of the wall or partition, within 150 mm of an angle formed by two adjoining walls, or horizontally/vertically in line with an accessory.","ref":"Reg 522.6.202","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a058","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"You chase a new cable vertically down a plastered brick wall to a socket-outlet. Which route complies with the prescribed zones without extra protection being needed from mechanical cover?","options":["Vertically 300 mm to the side of the socket","Diagonally from the ceiling corner to the socket","Horizontally 400 mm from the ceiling across the wall","Vertically in line with the socket-outlet box"],"answer":3,"explain":"Permitted zones are within 150 mm of the top of the wall, within 150 mm of a corner, or horizontally/vertically in line with the accessory. Diagonal runs and runs offset from the accessory are outside the zones.","ref":"Reg 522.6.202","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a059","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"In a dwelling, a cable without an earthed metallic covering is concealed less than 50 mm deep in a wall within a prescribed zone. What else is required?","options":["A label on the wall indicating the cable route","A minimum 4 mm² cpc","Additional protection by a 30 mA RCD","Nothing further – being in the zone is sufficient"],"answer":2,"explain":"Zones reduce the chance of penetration but do not prevent it. For cables in zones without earthed metallic covering, earthed conduit or mechanical protection, 522.6.202 requires 30 mA RCD additional protection (for installations not under skilled supervision, which includes dwellings).","ref":"Reg 522.6.202","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a060","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"A cable is run in a partition that has internal metal studs (other than fixings). Regardless of its depth, what does 522.6.203 require if the cable has no earthed metallic covering or mechanical protection?","options":["Installation only in the 150 mm zones","Additional protection by a 30 mA RCD","Supplementary bonding of the metal studs","Use of fire-resistant cable"],"answer":1,"explain":"Metal parts in a partition can damage a cable when screws or nails are driven in, whatever the depth. 522.6.203 therefore requires 30 mA RCD protection unless the cable is otherwise protected (earthed metallic covering, earthed conduit, etc.).","ref":"Reg 522.6.203","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a061","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"When a cable is installed through a fire-rated wall, the hole around it must be:","options":["Filled with any general-purpose expanding foam","Sealed to restore the fire resistance of the wall","Sealed only where the cable exceeds 10 mm²","Left open to allow the cable to dissipate heat"],"answer":1,"explain":"527.2 requires openings through elements of building construction to be sealed according to the fire resistance of the element, so the wiring system does not reduce the building's fire compartmentation.","ref":"Reg 527.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a062","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"A trunking passes through a fire-rated floor. Non-flame-propagating conduit or trunking need not be internally sealed if its internal cross-sectional area does not exceed:","options":["710 mm²","2500 mm²","1000 mm²","100 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wiring systems penetrating fire-rated elements must generally be internally sealed as well as externally. Non-flame-propagating systems with an internal csa not exceeding 710 mm² are exempt from internal sealing.","ref":"Reg 527.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a063","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"Why does 521.10.202 effectively rule out plastic clips or plastic trunking as the sole means of support for cables?","options":["Plastic supports cannot achieve the required IP rating","Plastic clips raise the operating temperature of the cable","Plastic trunking causes interference with data cables","Cables must not collapse early in a fire and entangle people"],"answer":3,"explain":"In a fire, plastic supports melt and cables can drop across escape routes and entangle people. 521.10.202 requires supports that resist premature collapse, e.g. metal clips or fixings.","ref":"Reg 521.10.202","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a064","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"Band I (e.g. data or bell) and Band II (230 V) circuits are to be installed in the same trunking. Which is acceptable?","options":["Run them together if the Band II circuits are RCD protected","Segregate them by a partition, or insulate all for the highest voltage","Run them together provided the Band I cables have a screen","Band I cables may never share any route with Band II cables"],"answer":1,"explain":"528.1 requires Band I and Band II circuits not to share a wiring system unless segregated (e.g. by a partition in the trunking) or every cable is insulated for the highest voltage present.","ref":"Reg 528.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a065","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"In an IP code, what does the FIRST characteristic numeral indicate?","options":["Protection against solid foreign objects","Protection against ingress of water","The maximum ambient temperature","Protection against mechanical impact"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first numeral (0–6) covers solid objects and access to hazardous parts; the second (0–8) covers water. Mechanical impact is covered separately by IK codes.","ref":"BS EN 60529 / Reg 416.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a066","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"On a new three-phase distribution circuit, the line conductors L1, L2 and L3 should be identified as:","options":["Brown, blue and grey","Red, yellow and blue","Brown, black and blue","Brown, black and grey"],"answer":3,"explain":"Harmonised colours: L1 brown, L2 black, L3 grey, neutral blue, protective conductor green-and-yellow. Red/yellow/blue is the pre-2004 scheme.","ref":"Table 51 / Section 514","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a067","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"The green-and-yellow colour combination may be used for:","options":["Any conductor provided it is sleeved at terminations","Protective conductors and functional earth only in data cabling","Protective conductors only","Neutral conductors in three-phase circuits"],"answer":2,"explain":"514.4.2 reserves the bi-colour combination green-and-yellow exclusively for identification of protective conductors; it must not be used for any other purpose.","ref":"Reg 514.4.2","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a068","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"When the blue conductor of a flat twin and cpc cable is used as a switched line in a lighting circuit, you must:","options":["Leave it blue, as colour doesn't matter in switch wiring","Tape it black to show it is a switched conductor","Mark it brown (or L) at its terminations","Sleeve it green-and-yellow at each end"],"answer":2,"explain":"Section 514 requires conductors to be identified by colour or marking. A blue conductor used as a line must be marked brown (or 'L') at its terminations so it is not mistaken for a neutral.","ref":"Section 514","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a069","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"Within domestic (household) premises, consumer units and similar switchgear assemblies must:","options":["Be installed at least 2 m above finished floor level","Be located outside the dwelling in a meter cupboard","Be of all-insulated plastic construction for shock protection","Have a non-combustible enclosure, or be in a non-combustible cabinet"],"answer":3,"explain":"421.1.201 requires non-combustible enclosures (e.g. ferrous metal) for consumer units in domestic premises, following fires originating from connections in plastic consumer units.","ref":"Reg 421.1.201","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a070","topic":"Selection & erection","q":"For an installation supplied directly from a public LV supply, what is the maximum voltage drop for a lighting circuit, per the Appendix 4 guidance, at 230 V?","options":["11.5 V (5 %)","9.2 V (4 %)","6.9 V (3 %)","4.6 V (2 %)"],"answer":2,"explain":"Appendix 4 gives 3 % for lighting and 5 % for other uses from a public LV supply. 230 x 0.03 = 6.9 V; for other circuits 230 x 0.05 = 11.5 V.","ref":"Appendix 4 (Section 6.4)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a071","topic":"Special locations","q":"In a bathroom (Section 701), zone 1 extends up to a height above the finished floor of:","options":["3.0 m in all cases, measured from the floor","2.5 m in all cases, measured from the bath rim","2.25 m (or the highest fixed shower head, if higher)","2.0 m, or the ceiling height if that is lower"],"answer":2,"explain":"Zone 1 is bounded by the finished floor and the higher of 2.25 m or the highest fixed shower head or water outlet. 2.5 m is arm's reach, not a zone limit.","ref":"Section 701","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a072","topic":"Special locations","q":"Zone 2 in a bathroom extends horizontally beyond the boundary of zone 1 by:","options":["1.2 m","0.3 m","3 m","0.6 m"],"answer":3,"explain":"Zone 2 extends 0.6 m horizontally from the zone 1 boundary (up to the zone 1 height limit). 3 m is the distance within which socket-outlets are generally not permitted.","ref":"Section 701","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a073","topic":"Special locations","q":"You are installing a new electric shower circuit in a domestic bathroom. What RCD protection does Section 701 require?","options":["30 mA RCD, as for every circuit in the location","None, because the shower is fixed equipment","An RCD only if the maximum Zs cannot be met","A 100 mA time-delayed RCD at the origin only"],"answer":0,"explain":"701.411.3.3 requires all circuits of the location to be provided with additional protection by one or more RCDs of 30 mA, including circuits for showers and circuits passing through the location.","ref":"Reg 701.411.3.3","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a074","topic":"Special locations","q":"In a room containing a bath or shower, socket-outlets (other than SELV and shaver supply units) are not permitted within what horizontal distance of the zone 1 boundary?","options":["0.6 m","1.2 m","2.25 m","3 m"],"answer":3,"explain":"Section 701 prohibits socket-outlets within 3 m horizontally of the zone 1 boundary, except SELV socket-outlets and shaver supply units complying with BS EN 61558-2-5.","ref":"Section 701","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a075","topic":"Special locations","q":"What is the minimum degree of protection for fixed equipment installed in bathroom zones 1 and 2 (no cleaning water jets)?","options":["IPX4","IPX7","IPX2","IPX5"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equipment in zones 1 and 2 needs at least IPX4; IPX5 where water jets are likely for cleaning. Equipment in zone 0 needs IPX7.","ref":"Section 701","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a076","topic":"Special locations","q":"Supplementary protective bonding in a bathroom may be omitted when which conditions are met?","options":["The installation is TN-S and the bathroom has a mechanical extractor fan","All final circuits meet ADS times, have 30 mA RCDs, and extraneous parts are main-bonded","The bathroom has no socket-outlets and all circuits are protected by Type B circuit-breakers of 32 A or less","All circuits have 100 mA RCD protection and all pipework is copper"],"answer":1,"explain":"701.415.2 allows supplementary bonding to be omitted if the installation has main bonding to which all extraneous-conductive-parts of the location are connected, and all final circuits of the location meet disconnection times and have 30 mA RCD protection.","ref":"Reg 701.415.2","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a077","topic":"Special locations","q":"Around a swimming pool basin, zone 1 extends horizontally from the rim of the basin by:","options":["2 m","3.5 m","1.5 m","0.6 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"Section 702: zone 1 extends 2 m horizontally from the rim and 2.5 m vertically; zone 2 extends a further 1.5 m beyond zone 1.","ref":"Section 702","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a078","topic":"Special locations","q":"On a construction site, a single-phase reduced low voltage (RLV) system uses a 110 V centre-tapped transformer. What is the maximum voltage to earth?","options":["55 V","25 V","110 V","63.5 V"],"answer":0,"explain":"The secondary centre tap is earthed, so each line is half the secondary voltage from earth: 110 / 2 = 55 V.","ref":"Section 704 / Reg 411.8","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a079","topic":"Special locations","q":"On site, 110 V industrial plugs and socket-outlets to BS EN 60309-2 are colour-coded:","options":["Blue","Yellow","White","Red"],"answer":1,"explain":"BS EN 60309-2 colour coding: yellow 110 V, blue 230 V, red 400 V.","ref":"Section 704 / BS EN 60309-2","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a080","topic":"Special locations","q":"On a construction site, a 230 V socket-outlet circuit rated 32 A supplies hand-held tools. If RLV, SELV and electrical separation are not used, what protection is required?","options":["A 300 mA RCD","A Type D circuit-breaker","No RCD, as ADS is enough","Additional protection by a 30 mA RCD"],"answer":3,"explain":"Section 704 requires socket-outlet circuits up to 32 A to be protected by RLV, SELV, electrical separation, or ADS with 30 mA RCD additional protection.","ref":"Section 704","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a081","topic":"Special locations","q":"In agricultural and horticultural premises, for protection against fire, circuits must be protected by an RCD with a rated residual operating current not exceeding:","options":["100 mA","300 mA","500 mA","30 mA"],"answer":1,"explain":"705.422.7 requires RCDs not exceeding 300 mA for fire protection in agricultural and horticultural premises, which often contain combustible materials such as hay and straw.","ref":"Reg 705.422.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a082","topic":"Special locations","q":"Caravan pitch socket-outlets (Section 708) must be installed with their lowest part at a height above ground of:","options":["At least 1.8 m","0.3 m to 1.0 m","0.5 m to 1.5 m","1.0 m to 2.0 m"],"answer":2,"explain":"Section 708 requires pitch socket-outlets to be between 0.5 m and 1.5 m above ground, to BS EN 60309-2, and at least IP44.","ref":"Section 708","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a083","topic":"Special locations","q":"How must each caravan pitch socket-outlet be protected?","options":["By one 30 mA RCD shared between up to six socket-outlets","By a single 100 mA time-delayed RCD at the site origin","Individually – its own overcurrent device and own 30 mA RCD","By the fuse or MCB inside each caravan's own consumer unit"],"answer":2,"explain":"Section 708 requires each socket-outlet to have individual overcurrent protection and individual 30 mA RCD protection, so a fault in one caravan does not disconnect others.","ref":"Section 708","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a084","topic":"Special locations","q":"On a caravan park supplied by PME, what earthing arrangement is used for the pitch socket-outlets?","options":["Unearthed – the pitch socket-outlets rely on RCD protection only","PME – the earth is extended to each pitch and connected to the caravan chassis","TT – the PME earth must not be connected to the pitch protective conductors","PME, plus a supplementary bond from each caravan to the water standpipe"],"answer":2,"explain":"ESQCR 2002 prohibits connecting a combined neutral and protective conductor to metalwork in a caravan or boat, and Section 708 requires the pitch protective conductors not to be connected to a PME earthing facility.","ref":"Section 708 / ESQCR 2002","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a085","topic":"Special locations","q":"For an outdoor EV charge point on a PME supply, one option to avoid TT earthing is a device that disconnects the charge point when the voltage between the circuit protective conductor and Earth exceeds:","options":["50 V r.m.s., within 0.4 s","25 V r.m.s., within 0.2 s","110 V r.m.s., within 1 s","70 V r.m.s., within 5 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"722.411.4.1 permits PME for an outdoor charge point where a device monitors the cpc-to-Earth voltage and disconnects (live conductors and protective conductor) within 5 s if it exceeds 70 V r.m.s. Other options include a TT arrangement.","ref":"Reg 722.411.4.1","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a086","topic":"Special locations","q":"What RCD type is acceptable for protecting a mode 3 EV charging point that has no built-in DC fault protection?","options":["Type B, or Type A with 6 mA DC detection","Type AC with a 30 mA rating","A 100 mA time-delayed S-type RCD","Type F only, with no other device"],"answer":0,"explain":"EV chargers can produce smooth DC fault currents that blind Type AC/A devices. Section 722 requires Type B, or Type A (or F) with equipment providing disconnection for DC fault currents above 6 mA.","ref":"Section 722","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a087","topic":"Special locations","q":"Why must a PV installation be treated as live on the DC side even after the AC isolator has been switched off?","options":["The DNO back-feeds the DC cabling via the meter","The AC isolator switches only the neutral conductor","The inverter stores charge for up to 24 hours","The modules generate whenever they're illuminated"],"answer":3,"explain":"PV modules produce a DC voltage whenever light falls on them, so opening the AC isolator does not make the DC cabling dead. Section 712 requires isolation on both AC and DC sides and appropriate warning labels.","ref":"Section 712","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a088","topic":"Special locations","q":"A small single-phase domestic PV inverter rated up to 16 A per phase is connected under EREC G98. What must be done regarding the DNO?","options":["Obtain written DNO approval before installation","Nothing – DNO notification is not required","Notify the HSE instead of the DNO","Notify the DNO within 28 days of commissioning"],"answer":3,"explain":"G98 covers micro-generators up to 16 A per phase and uses a 'connect and notify' process: the installer notifies the DNO within 28 days of commissioning. Larger generation falls under G99, which needs prior approval.","ref":"ENA EREC G98","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a089","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Under BS 7671:2018+A2:2022, Regulation 443.4.1 requires protection against transient overvoltages where the consequences of an overvoltage could:","options":["Loss of a television signal","Serious injury to, or loss of, human life","Tripping of an RCD","Minor inconvenience to a single occupant"],"answer":1,"explain":"A2:2022 443.4.1 requires SPD protection where the consequences could (i) result in serious injury to, or loss of, human life; (ii) result in failure of a safety service; or (iii) result in significant financial or data loss. For all other cases SPDs shall be fitted unless the owner declines them and accepts the risk.","ref":"Reg 443.4.1 (A2:2022)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a090","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Under BS 7671:2018+A2:2022, for an installation (including a dwelling) that does not fall into any of the categories listed in 443.4.1, what applies regarding SPDs?","options":["No SPD is needed unless a CRL risk calculation shows one is required","SPDs shall be fitted unless the owner declines them and accepts the risk","SPDs are never required in single dwelling units","An AFDD must be fitted in place of an SPD"],"answer":1,"explain":"A2:2022 removed both the risk-assessment (CRL calculation) route and the single-dwelling exemption from 443.4. For all cases not covered by 443.4.1 (i)-(iii), SPDs shall be fitted unless the owner of the installation declines such protection and accepts the risk of damage to wiring and equipment as tolerable.","ref":"Reg 443.4.1 (A2:2022)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a091","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"A Type 1 SPD is primarily intended for:","options":["Local protection of individual socket-outlets close to sensitive equipment","Installations exposed to direct lightning current, e.g. with a lightning protection system","Replacing the RCD at the origin of a TT installation fed by an overhead line","Protecting against earth leakage currents from electronic equipment"],"answer":1,"explain":"Type 1 SPDs handle partial lightning currents (10/350 µs), typically at the origin where there is an LPS. Type 2 (8/20 µs) are installed at distribution boards; Type 3 give local protection near sensitive equipment.","ref":"Section 534","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a092","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"To be effective, the total length of the connecting conductors to an SPD (line to SPD plus SPD to earth) should preferably not exceed:","options":["1.5 m","3 m","5 m","0.5 m"],"answer":3,"explain":"Long connecting leads add inductive voltage drop that reduces SPD effectiveness. Section 534 states the total lead length should preferably not exceed 0.5 m (and in any case not exceed 1 m).","ref":"Reg 534.4.8","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a093","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Under BS 7671:2018+A2:2022, AFDDs SHALL be installed for single-phase a.c. final circuits supplying socket-outlets up to 32 A in which of the following?","options":["Detached garages","All new owner-occupied houses","Industrial workshops","Houses in multiple occupation (HMOs)"],"answer":3,"explain":"421.1.7 (A2) makes AFDDs mandatory for such circuits in higher-risk residential buildings, HMOs, purpose-built student accommodation and care homes. For all other premises they are recommended.","ref":"Reg 421.1.7 (A2:2022)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a094","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Where an AFDD is installed to protect a final circuit, it should be placed:","options":["Inside the appliance plug","At the origin of the circuit to be protected","At the meter position before the main switch","At the furthest socket-outlet"],"answer":1,"explain":"421.1.7 requires the AFDD to be placed at the origin of the circuit, i.e. in the consumer unit or distribution board, so the whole circuit is monitored.","ref":"Reg 421.1.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a095","topic":"Surge & AFDD","q":"Which fault can an AFDD detect that neither an MCB nor an RCD would respond to?","options":["A bolted short-circuit between line and neutral","An earth fault with a residual current of 30 mA","A series arc at a loose terminal carrying load current","An overload of 1.45 times the device rating"],"answer":2,"explain":"A series arc (e.g. a loose terminal or damaged conductor) draws no more than load current and no residual current, so MCBs and RCDs do not see it. AFDDs analyse the current waveform for arc signatures.","ref":"Reg 421.1.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a096","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Under Regulation 14 of the Electricity at Work Regulations 1989, live working is permitted only when:","options":["It is unreasonable to be dead, reasonable to work live, and suitable precautions are taken","The circuit is protected by a 30 mA RCD and the worker is a skilled person","The voltage is below 400 V and insulated gloves are worn at all times","The customer has insisted in writing that the supply must remain on"],"answer":0,"explain":"Regulation 14 sets three conditions, all of which must be met: it is unreasonable in all the circumstances for the equipment to be dead; it is reasonable for the person to work on or near it live; and suitable precautions (including PPE where necessary) are taken.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 14","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a097","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Which Regulation of the EAWR 1989 requires persons to have the technical knowledge or experience to prevent danger (or to be under appropriate supervision)?","options":["Regulation 12","Regulation 16","Regulation 13","Regulation 4"],"answer":1,"explain":"Regulation 16 deals with competence to prevent danger and injury. Reg 4 covers systems, work activities and protective equipment; Reg 12 means for cutting off supply and isolation; Reg 13 precautions for work on equipment made dead.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 16","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a098","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Regulation 12 of the EAWR 1989 requires suitable means to be available for:","options":["Adequate working space, access and lighting","Cutting off the supply and isolating equipment","Earthing or other precautions against charge","Protecting systems against excess current"],"answer":1,"explain":"Regulation 12 requires means for cutting off the supply and for isolation. Excess current protection is Reg 11; earthing is Reg 8; working space, access and lighting is Reg 15.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 12","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a099","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Regulation 13 of the EAWR 1989 requires that when equipment has been made dead for work:","options":["A 30 mA RCD is fitted to the circuit before work starts","The work is always carried out by a minimum of two people","It is inspected and tested again every twelve months thereafter","Precautions are taken to stop it becoming charged during the work"],"answer":3,"explain":"Regulation 13 covers precautions for work on equipment made dead, e.g. securing isolation with locks, posting warning notices and proving dead, so the equipment cannot be re-energised during the work.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 13","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a100","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Under section 7 of the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, employees have a duty to:","options":["Prepare and revise the company's written health and safety policy","Take reasonable care for themselves and others, and co-operate with their employer","Carry out and record all the risk assessments for the site themselves","Provide and maintain safe plant and safe systems of work for all staff"],"answer":1,"explain":"Section 7 places duties on employees. Providing safe plant and systems of work is an employer duty under section 2.","ref":"HSWA 1974 s.7","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a101","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"According to HSE GS38, test probes should have an exposed metal tip of not more than:","options":["6 mm, preferably 4 mm, with finger guards","1 mm in all cases, with no exceptions","4 mm (preferably 2 mm), or be spring-loaded","10 mm, to allow contact with recessed terminals"],"answer":2,"explain":"GS38 recommends a maximum 4 mm exposed tip, preferably 2 mm or less or spring-loaded retractable, to reduce the risk of accidental short circuits, together with finger barriers and suitably protected leads.","ref":"HSE GS38","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a102","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Why does GS38 advise against using a multimeter to prove a circuit dead?","options":["Multimeters are unable to measure a.c. voltages above 50 V","Selecting the wrong range or function can give a false 'dead' reading","Multimeters may only be used for insulation resistance testing","GS38 prohibits multimeters from being taken onto building sites"],"answer":1,"explain":"A multimeter can easily be left on the wrong setting (e.g. current or resistance), giving a false reading or causing a fault. GS38 recommends a proprietary two-pole voltage indicator for proving dead.","ref":"HSE GS38","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a103","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"In the safe isolation procedure, after testing the circuit and finding it dead, what must you do next?","options":["Switch the circuit back on briefly to confirm the tester works","Re-prove the voltage indicator on a known live source or proving unit","Remove your lock so others can use the circuit when needed","Start work immediately, as the circuit has been proved dead"],"answer":1,"explain":"The indicator is proved before and after the test. If it failed during the test, a 'dead' reading would be false; re-proving confirms the dead result was genuine.","ref":"HSE GS38 / safe isolation procedure","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a104","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"Which sequence is correct for safe isolation?","options":["Isolate, test dead, identify the circuit, lock off, then prove the tester","Identify, isolate, secure (lock and label), prove tester, test dead, re-prove tester","Identify, test dead, isolate, remove the fuse and keep it, then start work","Prove tester, test dead, isolate, lock off and label, then re-prove the tester"],"answer":1,"explain":"The circuit must be identified, isolated and secured before testing so the test confirms the isolation. The voltage indicator is proved on a known source before and after testing dead.","ref":"Safe isolation procedure","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a105","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"When proving dead at a single-phase isolator, which combinations should be tested?","options":["Line–neutral, line–earth and neutral–earth","Neutral–earth only, at the isolator terminals","Line–neutral only, using a two-pole tester","Line–earth only, as neutral is at earth potential"],"answer":0,"explain":"Testing all three combinations detects a live conductor however it is connected, including a borrowed neutral or a line connected to the wrong terminal.","ref":"Safe isolation procedure","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a106","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"On a three-phase and neutral isolator, how many tests are needed to prove dead between all conductors?","options":["10","6","4","7"],"answer":0,"explain":"Line–line: L1–L2, L1–L3, L2–L3 = 3. Line–neutral: 3. Line–earth: 3. Neutral–earth: 1. Total 3 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 10.","ref":"Safe isolation procedure","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a107","topic":"Safety law & safe isolation","q":"You have isolated a circuit at a consumer unit in an occupied house. Which is the best way to secure the isolation?","options":["Remove the consumer unit cover so it's obvious work is going on","Lock it off with your own padlock and key, and post a warning notice","Switch off the MCB and put insulating tape over the toggle","Switch off and ask the homeowner not to touch the consumer unit at all"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tape or verbal instructions do not prevent re-energisation. A lock-off device with a padlock under the worker's control, plus a warning notice, meets the intent of EAWR Reg 13.","ref":"EAWR 1989 Reg 13 / safe isolation","level":"AM2E","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a108","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"The requirement of Part P of the Building Regulations is that reasonable provision shall be made in the design and installation of electrical installations to protect persons:","options":["From excessive energy use and high electricity bills","Operating, maintaining or altering them, from fire or injury","From electromagnetic interference caused by the wiring","Against lightning strikes to the building structure"],"answer":1,"explain":"Requirement P1 is about protecting people operating, maintaining or altering the installation from fire or injury. It applies to electrical installations in and around dwellings.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a109","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"Which of the following electrical work in a dwelling is notifiable under Part P in both England and Wales?","options":["Changing a lamp","Installing a new circuit","Replacing a damaged socket-outlet faceplate like-for-like in a hallway","Replacing a flexible cord to a pendant"],"answer":1,"explain":"In both England and Wales, installing a new circuit and replacing a consumer unit are notifiable, as is certain work in special locations. Like-for-like replacement of accessories outside special locations is not notifiable.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P (England and Wales)","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a110","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"A homeowner asks you to replace their consumer unit. Under Part P, this work is:","options":["Non-notifiable if the new unit has the same number of ways","Exempt if an MWC is issued","Notifiable only if the house is rented","Notifiable, via a competent person scheme or Building Control"],"answer":3,"explain":"Consumer unit replacement is notifiable in England and Wales. It must be self-certified through a registered competent person scheme, or notified to Building Control before work starts. An EIC, not an MWC, is issued.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a111","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"An electrician who is NOT registered with a competent person scheme wants to carry out notifiable work in a dwelling. What must they do?","options":["Have the homeowner sign a disclaimer accepting responsibility","Notify Building Control before starting, so the work can be inspected","Nothing further, provided they hold the 2391 qualification","Carry out the work and then issue an EICR on completion"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without competent person scheme registration, notifiable work must go through Building Control (e.g. a building notice) before work begins. Qualifications alone do not allow self-certification.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a112","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"When a registered competent person completes notifiable work, how is Building Control informed?","options":["The homeowner must apply to Building Control for an inspection","The DNO notifies Building Control when the meter is next read","The scheme notifies Building Control; the occupier gets a compliance certificate","The electrician posts a copy of the EIC to the local council"],"answer":2,"explain":"Competent person scheme members self-certify: they notify via their scheme, which informs the local authority, and the occupier receives a compliance certificate as well as the EIC.","ref":"Building Regulations Part P","level":"EWA","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a113","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"You add a new radial circuit for a kitchen appliance. Which certificate should be issued?","options":["Electrical Installation Certificate (EIC)","Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate (MWC)","No certificate for a single circuit","Electrical Installation Condition Report (EICR)"],"answer":0,"explain":"A new circuit requires an EIC with a schedule of inspections and schedule of test results. An MWC is only for additions or alterations to an existing circuit that do not include a new circuit.","ref":"Chapter 64 / Appendix 6","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a114","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"You add one socket-outlet to an existing ring final circuit (not a new circuit). Which certificate is appropriate?","options":["Minor Electrical Installation Works Certificate (MWC)","Electrical Installation Condition Report (EICR)","Electrical Danger Notification only","Electrical Installation Certificate (EIC)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The MWC covers additions and alterations to an existing circuit that do not include the provision of a new circuit, such as adding a socket-outlet or lighting point.","ref":"Chapter 64 / Appendix 6","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a115","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"On a three-signature EIC, the three signatories are responsible for:","options":["Design, DNO approval and Building Control","Inspection, testing and client acceptance","Design, construction, and inspection and testing","Construction, testing and scheme assessment"],"answer":2,"explain":"The EIC has separate declarations for design, construction and inspection and testing. Where one person is responsible for all three, a single-signature form may be used.","ref":"Chapter 64 / Appendix 6","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a116","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"During an EICR you find a socket-outlet with a broken faceplate exposing live terminals. What classification code applies?","options":["C3 – Improvement recommended","C2 – Potentially dangerous, urgent remedial action required","FI – Further investigation required","C1 – Danger present, immediate remedial action required"],"answer":3,"explain":"Accessible live parts present immediate danger, so C1. The inspector should take immediate action (e.g. make safe or isolate with the duty holder's agreement) as well as recording it.","ref":"GN3 / EICR classification codes","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a117","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"On an EICR, what does code C2 mean?","options":["Further investigation required without delay","Improvement recommended","Danger present – immediate remedial action required","Potentially dangerous – urgent remedial action required"],"answer":3,"explain":"C1 = danger present; C2 = potentially dangerous, urgent remedial action required; C3 = improvement recommended; FI = further investigation required without delay.","ref":"GN3 / EICR classification codes","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a118","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"An EICR records several C3 observations and one FI. What should the overall assessment be?","options":["Satisfactory with recommendations","Satisfactory","Unsatisfactory","Not applicable until the FI is resolved"],"answer":2,"explain":"Any C1, C2 or FI makes the overall assessment unsatisfactory. C3s alone do not make an installation unsatisfactory.","ref":"GN3 / EICR","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a119","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"What is the typical recommended maximum interval between periodic inspections for an owner-occupied domestic installation?","options":["5 years or on change of occupancy","3 years","1 year","10 years or on change of occupancy"],"answer":3,"explain":"GN3 suggests up to 10 years (or change of occupancy) for owner-occupied domestic premises; rented domestic, commercial and many other premises are typically 5 years.","ref":"GN3 (recommended maximum intervals)","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a120","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"What is the typical recommended maximum interval between periodic inspections for an industrial installation?","options":["10 years","5 years","3 years","1 year"],"answer":2,"explain":"GN3 guidance: industrial 3 years, commercial 5 years, domestic 10 years (rented domestic 5 years).","ref":"GN3 (recommended maximum intervals)","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"a121","topic":"Part P & certification","q":"Under the Electrical Safety Standards in the Private Rented Sector (England) Regulations 2020, landlords must have the installation inspected and tested at intervals of no more than:","options":["10 years","5 years","1 year","3 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"The 2020 regulations require private landlords in England to ensure inspection and testing by a qualified person at intervals of no more than 5 years, and to give tenants a copy of the report.","ref":"Electrical Safety Standards in the Private Rented Sector (England) Regulations 2020","level":"2391","area":"Regs & theory"},{"id":"b001","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 3 kW immersion heater is connected to a 230 V supply. What current does it draw?","options":["0.08 A","17.63 A","13.04 A","6.52 A"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Formula: I = P / V\n2. Substitute: I = 3000 / 230\n3. Result: I = 13.04 A\n4. Check: P = V x I = 230 x 13.04 = 2999 W (approx 3 kW). Dividing V by P (0.08 A) is the classic upside-down mistake.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b002","topic":"Electrical science","q":"What is the hot resistance of the element in a 3 kW, 230 V immersion heater?","options":["13.04 Ω","17.63 Ω","0.06 Ω","690 Ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: P = V²/R, so R = V² / P\n2. Substitute: R = 230² / 3000 = 52 900 / 3000\n3. Result: R = 17.63 Ω\n4. Check with Ohm's law: I = 230 / 17.63 = 13.04 A, and 230 x 13.04 = 3000 W.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b003","topic":"Electrical science","q":"Three resistors of 12 Ω, 18 Ω and 30 Ω are connected in SERIES across 230 V. What current flows?","options":["39.61 A","0.26 A","19.17 A","3.83 A"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Series: RT = R1 + R2 + R3 = 12 + 18 + 30 = 60 Ω\n2. I = V / RT = 230 / 60\n3. Result: I = 3.83 A\n4. 39.61 A would be the answer if you had wrongly treated them as parallel (5.81 Ω).","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b004","topic":"Electrical science","q":"Two heating elements of 20 Ω and 30 Ω are connected in PARALLEL. What is the total resistance?","options":["12.00 Ω","50.00 Ω","25.00 Ω","0.083 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Parallel: 1/RT = 1/R1 + 1/R2\n2. 1/RT = 1/20 + 1/30 = 0.05 + 0.0333 = 0.0833\n3. RT = 1 / 0.0833 = 12.00 Ω (or product over sum: (20 x 30)/(20 + 30) = 600/50 = 12 Ω)\n4. Leaving the answer as 0.083 means you forgot to take the reciprocal; total parallel resistance is always less than the smallest branch.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b005","topic":"Electrical science","q":"Resistors of 10 Ω, 20 Ω and 20 Ω are connected in parallel across 230 V. What is the total current drawn?","options":["46.00 A","4.60 A","23.00 A","13.80 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. 1/RT = 1/10 + 1/20 + 1/20 = 0.10 + 0.05 + 0.05 = 0.20\n2. RT = 1 / 0.20 = 5.00 Ω\n3. I = V / RT = 230 / 5.00 = 46.00 A\n4. Check: branch currents 23 + 11.5 + 11.5 = 46 A. 4.60 A is the series answer.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b006","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A cable run has a total loop resistance of 0.25 Ω and carries 32 A. What power is lost as heat in the cable?","options":["256 W","8 W","64 W","4096 W"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: P = I²R\n2. Substitute: P = 32² x 0.25 = 1024 x 0.25\n3. Result: P = 256 W\n4. 8 W is just I x R (that is the volt drop in volts, not watts); 4096 W comes from I²/R.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b007","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A single-phase motor draws 8 A at 230 V with a power factor of 0.8. What is its true (active) power?","options":["1840 W","2300 W","1472 W","1178 W"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Apparent power S = V x I = 230 x 8 = 1840 VA\n2. True power P = V x I x pf = 1840 x 0.8\n3. Result: P = 1472 W\n4. 1840 W ignores the power factor; 2300 W divides by pf instead of multiplying.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b008","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 4.6 kW single-phase load operates at 230 V with a power factor of 0.85. What is the design current?","options":["23.53 A","20.00 A","17.00 A","13.58 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: I = P / (V x pf)\n2. Substitute: I = 4600 / (230 x 0.85) = 4600 / 195.5\n3. Result: I = 23.53 A\n4. 20.00 A ignores pf; 17.00 A multiplies by pf; 13.58 A wrongly brings in √3 for a single-phase load. A poor pf always INCREASES current.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b009","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 22 kW three-phase motor runs on a 400 V supply at a power factor of 0.9. What is the line current?","options":["61.11 A","35.28 A","31.75 A","20.37 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: IL = P / (√3 x VL x pf)\n2. √3 x 400 x 0.9 = 1.732 x 400 x 0.9 = 623.5\n3. IL = 22 000 / 623.5 = 35.28 A\n4. 61.11 A is the single-phase formula (no √3); 31.75 A forgets the pf; 20.37 A uses 3 instead of √3.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b010","topic":"Electrical science","q":"An 18 kW balanced three-phase heater bank (unity power factor) is supplied at 400 V. What is the line current?","options":["25.98 A","45.00 A","78.26 A","15.00 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: IL = P / (√3 x VL)  (pf = 1)\n2. √3 x 400 = 692.8\n3. IL = 18 000 / 692.8 = 25.98 A\n4. Cross-check per phase: 6 kW per phase / 230 V = 26.09 A - near enough the same (the exact phase voltage is 400/√3 = 230.9 V).","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b011","topic":"Electrical science","q":"In a star-connected supply the line voltage is 400 V. What is the phase (line-to-neutral) voltage?","options":["230.9 V","692.8 V","133.3 V","200.0 V"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. In star: VL = √3 x VP, so VP = VL / √3\n2. VP = 400 / 1.732\n3. Result: VP = 230.9 V (nominally 230 V)\n4. Multiplying by √3 (692.8 V) is the wrong direction.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b012","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 50 m length of 2.5 mm² copper conductor measures 0.37 Ω. Using proportion only, what would be the resistance of 100 m of 1.5 mm² copper conductor?","options":["0.44 Ω","0.74 Ω","0.22 Ω","1.23 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. R is proportional to length and inversely proportional to csa: R2 = R1 x (L2/L1) x (A1/A2)\n2. Length factor = 100/50 = 2; csa factor = 2.5/1.5 = 1.667\n3. R2 = 0.37 x 2 x 1.667 = 1.23 Ω\n4. 0.44 Ω inverts the csa ratio - a smaller conductor must have HIGHER resistance.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b013","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A copper conductor measures 0.50 Ω at 20 °C. Taking the temperature coefficient as 0.004 per °C, what is its resistance at its 70 °C operating temperature?","options":["0.64 Ω","0.52 Ω","0.42 Ω","0.60 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Formula: Rt = R20 x (1 + α x Δt)\n2. Temperature rise Δt = 70 - 20 = 50 °C\n3. Rt = 0.50 x (1 + 0.004 x 50) = 0.50 x 1.20 = 0.60 Ω\n4. This is where the 1.2 multiplier used for 70 °C PVC cables in Zs calculations comes from. 0.64 Ω wrongly uses 70 °C as the rise.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b014","topic":"Electrical science","q":"A 3 kW heater runs for 2.5 hours. Electricity costs 28p per kWh. What is the running cost?","options":["£2.10","£0.84","£21.00","£0.34"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Energy = P (kW) x t (h) = 3 x 2.5 = 7.5 kWh\n2. Cost = 7.5 x 28p = 210p\n3. Result: £2.10\n4. £0.84 forgets the time (3 x 28p).","ref":"Electrical science","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b015","topic":"Electrical science","q":"In a perfectly balanced three-phase, four-wire system with purely resistive loads, what current flows in the neutral?","options":["The same as one line current","Zero","√3 times one line current","Three times one line current"],"answer":1,"explain":"With equal currents 120° apart, the phasor sum of the three line currents is zero, so no neutral current flows. Neutral current appears when the phases are unbalanced (or with triplen harmonics).","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b016","topic":"Electrical science","q":"Power factor is defined as the ratio of:","options":["True power (W) to apparent power (VA)","Apparent power (VA) to true power (W)","Reactive power (var) to true power (W)","Voltage to current"],"answer":0,"explain":"pf = P / S = kW / kVA. It is 1 for purely resistive loads and less than 1 for inductive loads such as motors, which draw more current for the same useful power.","ref":"Electrical science","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b017","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 9.2 kW, 230 V electric shower is to be installed. What is the design current Ib?","options":["23.00 A","13.28 A","50.00 A","40.00 A"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Formula: Ib = P / V\n2. Substitute: Ib = 9200 / 230\n3. Result: Ib = 40.00 A\n4. Then choose In ≥ Ib (e.g. a 40 A device). 23 A wrongly uses 400 V; the √3 answer wrongly treats it as three-phase.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"EWA","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b018","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A circuit protected by a 32 A MCB runs in an ambient of 35 °C (Ca = 0.94) and is grouped with another circuit (Cg = 0.80). What minimum tabulated current-carrying capacity (It) is required?","options":["42.55 A","24.06 A","34.04 A","40.00 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / (Ca x Cg)\n2. Ca x Cg = 0.94 x 0.80 = 0.752\n3. It ≥ 32 / 0.752 = 42.55 A\n4. Correction factors are DIVIDED into In, so It always comes out bigger than In. 24.06 A multiplies instead.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b019","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A radial circuit protected by a 20 A MCB passes through a loft where the cable is totally surrounded by thermal insulation for 1.2 m (Ci = 0.5). No other factors apply. What minimum It is required?","options":["40.00 A","10.00 A","20.00 A","27.59 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / Ci\n2. Substitute: It ≥ 20 / 0.5\n3. Result: It ≥ 40.00 A\n4. Ci of 0.5 applies because the cable is totally surrounded by insulation for more than 0.5 m. 10 A multiplies; 27.59 A wrongly uses the BS 3036 factor.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b020","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A circuit is protected by a 30 A BS 3036 semi-enclosed (rewireable) fuse. No other correction factors apply. What minimum It is required?","options":["21.75 A","41.38 A","30.00 A","37.50 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / Cc, with Cc = 0.725 for BS 3036 fuses\n2. Substitute: It ≥ 30 / 0.725\n3. Result: It ≥ 41.38 A\n4. 21.75 A multiplies instead of divides - that would undersize the cable.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b021","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 20 A BS 3036 fuse protects a cable in a 40 °C ambient (Ca = 0.87). What minimum It is required? (Cc for BS 3036 = 0.725)","options":["31.71 A","12.61 A","22.99 A","27.59 A"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / (Ca x Cc)\n2. Ca x Cc = 0.87 x 0.725 = 0.6308\n3. It ≥ 20 / 0.6308 = 31.71 A\n4. The other answers either multiply (12.61 A) or apply only one of the two factors.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b022","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 32 A MCB protects a cable where Ca = 0.94 and Cg = 0.80. For the installation method used, the tabulated ratings are: 2.5 mm² 27 A, 4 mm² 37 A, 6 mm² 47 A, 10 mm² 64 A. What is the smallest suitable cable?","options":["4 mm²","2.5 mm²","10 mm²","6 mm²"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. It ≥ In / (Ca x Cg) = 32 / (0.94 x 0.80) = 32 / 0.752\n2. It ≥ 42.55 A\n3. From the values given: 4 mm² (37 A) is too small; 6 mm² (47 A) is the first that is ≥ 42.55 A\n4. Choosing 4 mm² is the mistake of comparing In (32 A) directly with the table and ignoring the factors.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b023","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 6 mm² cable has a tabulated rating It of 47 A. It is installed with Ca = 0.94 and Cg = 0.80 and protected by a 32 A MCB. What is its effective current-carrying capacity Iz, and is the device suitable?","options":["35.3 A - suitable, as Iz ≥ In","62.5 A - suitable, as Iz ≥ In","47.0 A - suitable, as Iz ≥ In","35.3 A - not suitable, as Iz < In"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: Iz = It x Ca x Cg (factors multiply when going from table to real capacity)\n2. Iz = 47 x 0.94 x 0.80 = 35.34 A\n3. Compare: 35.3 A ≥ 32 A, so In ≤ Iz is satisfied\n4. Dividing (62.5 A) is the reverse of the correct direction.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b024","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"A 16 A MCB protects a circuit with Ca = 0.87, Cg = 0.70, and a section totally surrounded by thermal insulation for over 0.5 m (Ci = 0.5). What minimum It is required?","options":["4.87 A","52.55 A","26.27 A","36.78 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: It ≥ In / (Ca x Cg x Ci)\n2. Ca x Cg x Ci = 0.87 x 0.70 x 0.5 = 0.3045\n3. It ≥ 16 / 0.3045 = 52.55 A\n4. 26.27 A forgets Ci; 36.78 A forgets Cg. Thermal insulation alone can double the cable size needed.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b025","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"Which relationship must be satisfied when selecting a protective device for overload protection of a cable?","options":["Ib ≤ In ≤ Iz","Iz ≤ In ≤ Ib","In ≤ Ib ≤ Iz","Ib ≤ Iz ≤ In"],"answer":0,"explain":"The device rating must be at least the design current (In ≥ Ib) and no greater than the cable's effective current-carrying capacity (In ≤ Iz).","ref":"Chapter 43","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b026","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"Why is a correction factor of 0.725 applied when a cable is protected by a BS 3036 semi-enclosed fuse?","options":["Because BS 3036 fuses run hotter than MCBs","Because the fuse needs about twice its rating to operate, and 1.45 ÷ 2 = 0.725 keeps the cable within its overload limit","Because the fuse element is 72.5% efficient","Because BS 3036 fuses have a lower breaking capacity"],"answer":1,"explain":"Overload protection requires the conventional operating current I2 ≤ 1.45 Iz. A BS 3036 fuse has a fusing factor of about 2, so the cable must be derated by 1.45/2 = 0.725.","ref":"Cable sizing","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b027","topic":"Cable sizing","q":"When does the correction factor Ci of 0.5 apply to a cable in thermal insulation?","options":["Whenever the cable touches thermal insulation on one side","Only when the cable passes through insulation for less than 0.1 m","When the cable is totally surrounded by thermal insulation for a length of more than 0.5 m","Only for cables in conduit in insulated walls"],"answer":2,"explain":"0.5 is the factor for a cable totally surrounded over more than 0.5 m. Shorter lengths totally surrounded use a less severe factor (Table 52.2); a cable touching insulation on one side uses the relevant reference installation method instead.","ref":"Reg 523.9","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b028","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A 30 m radial power circuit in 2.5 mm² cable (18 mV/A/m) has a design current of 20 A. What is the voltage drop, and does it comply with the 5% limit for a public supply?","options":["10.8 V - exceeds the 6.9 V limit","1.08 V - within the limit","108 V - exceeds the limit","10.8 V - within the 11.5 V limit"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Formula: VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L) / 1000\n2. Substitute: VD = (18 x 20 x 30) / 1000 = 10800 / 1000\n3. Result: VD = 10.8 V (4.7%)\n4. Limit for 'other' circuits = 5% of 230 = 11.5 V, so it complies. 6.9 V is the lighting limit.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b029","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A lighting circuit is 35 m long in 1.5 mm² cable (29 mV/A/m), protected by a 10 A MCB, with a design current of 6 A. What is the voltage drop and does it comply?","options":["10.15 V - fails the 6.9 V limit","6.09 V - complies with the 6.9 V limit","6.09 V - fails the 3% limit","0.61 V - complies"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Formula: VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L) / 1000 - use Ib, not In\n2. Substitute: VD = (29 x 6 x 35) / 1000 = 6090 / 1000\n3. Result: VD = 6.09 V (2.65%)\n4. Lighting limit is 3% of 230 = 6.9 V, so it complies. 10.15 V comes from wrongly using the 10 A device rating.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b030","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A 40 A shower circuit is wired in 6 mm² cable (7.3 mV/A/m). What is the maximum length that keeps voltage drop within the 5% limit (230 V supply)?","options":["39.4 m","23.6 m","68.5 m","3.94 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Allowed VD = 5% of 230 = 11.5 V\n2. Rearrange VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L)/1000 → L = (VD x 1000) / (mV/A/m x Ib)\n3. L = (11.5 x 1000) / (7.3 x 40) = 11 500 / 292\n4. Result: L = 39.4 m. 23.6 m uses the 3% lighting limit; 68.5 m uses 5% of 400 V.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b031","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A three-phase 400 V circuit is 60 m long in 4 mm² cable with a three-phase voltage drop figure of 9.5 mV/A/m. Ib = 25 A. What is the voltage drop and does it meet the 5% limit?","options":["24.68 V - fails (limit 20 V)","14.25 V - fails (limit 11.5 V)","14.25 V - complies (limit 20 V)","8.23 V - complies (limit 20 V)"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L) / 1000 using the three-phase mV/A/m value\n2. VD = (9.5 x 25 x 60) / 1000 = 14.25 V\n3. Limit = 5% of 400 V = 20 V\n4. 14.25 V < 20 V so it complies. The three-phase mV/A/m value already includes √3 - do not multiply again, and do not judge against the 230 V limit.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b032","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A ring final circuit in 2.5 mm² cable (18 mV/A/m) has a total ring length of 60 m. A 26 A load is connected at the midpoint of the ring. What is the approximate voltage drop?","options":["28.08 V","7.02 V","14.04 V","3.51 V"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. At the midpoint the load is fed by two legs of 30 m in parallel, which is equivalent to one conductor of 60 / 4 = 15 m\n2. VD = (mV/A/m x Ib x L) / 1000 / 4 = (18 x 26 x 60) / 1000 / 4\n3. = 28.08 / 4\n4. Result: VD = 7.02 V. Forgetting to divide by 4 gives 28.08 V.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b033","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"A 32 m radial in 4 mm² cable (11 mV/A/m) carries a design current of 28 A on a 230 V supply. What percentage voltage drop is this?","options":["4.29%","2.46%","9.86%","0.43%"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. VD = (11 x 28 x 32) / 1000 = 9.856 V\n2. Percentage = (VD / 230) x 100 = (9.856 / 230) x 100\n3. Result: 4.29%\n4. Within the 5% limit for power circuits. Dividing by 400 V is wrong for a single-phase circuit.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b034","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"For an installation supplied from a public low-voltage distribution system at 230 V, what are the usual voltage drop limits?","options":["Lighting 5% (11.5 V); other uses 3% (6.9 V)","Lighting 3% (6.9 V); other uses 5% (11.5 V)","Lighting 4% (9.2 V); other uses 4% (9.2 V)","Lighting 6% (13.8 V); other uses 8% (18.4 V)"],"answer":1,"explain":"Appendix 4 gives 3% for lighting and 5% for other uses where the supply is from a public LV network. At 230 V that is 6.9 V and 11.5 V.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b035","topic":"Voltage drop","q":"When calculating voltage drop for a radial circuit, which current should be used?","options":["The protective device rating In","The tabulated rating It","The design current Ib","The prospective fault current"],"answer":2,"explain":"Voltage drop depends on the current the circuit will actually carry, so it is calculated using Ib. Using In gives a pessimistic answer and can lead to oversizing.","ref":"Appendix 4","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b036","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A fault current of 1000 A is cleared in 0.1 s. Using the adiabatic equation with k = 115 (70 °C PVC copper), what is the minimum csa of protective conductor?","options":["2.75 mm²","0.87 mm²","2.21 mm²","869.57 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: S = √(I²t) / k\n2. I²t = 1000² x 0.1 = 100 000 A²s\n3. √100 000 = 316.2\n4. S = 316.2 / 115 = 2.75 mm²\n5. 0.87 mm² comes from using I x t instead of √(I²t); forgetting the square root gives 869.57.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b037","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A cpc incorporated in a 70 °C PVC cable (k = 115) must withstand a fault of 2000 A for 0.04 s. What is the minimum calculated csa and the smallest standard size?","options":["3.48 mm², so 4 mm² is needed","3.48 mm², so 2.5 mm² is adequate","0.70 mm², so 1.0 mm² is adequate","12.10 mm², so 16 mm² is needed"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. S = √(I²t) / k\n2. I²t = 2000² x 0.04 = 160 000 A²s; √160 000 = 400\n3. S = 400 / 115 = 3.48 mm²\n4. Always round UP to the next standard size: 2.5 mm² is below 3.48 mm², so 4 mm² is the minimum.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b038","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A separate 70 °C thermoplastic-insulated copper cpc, not bunched with cables (k = 143), must carry 1500 A for 0.4 s. What minimum csa is required?","options":["6.63 mm² - use 10 mm²","6.63 mm² - use 6 mm²","8.25 mm² - use 10 mm²","4.20 mm² - use 6 mm²"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. S = √(I²t) / k\n2. I²t = 1500² x 0.4 = 900 000 A²s; √900 000 = 948.7\n3. S = 948.7 / 143 = 6.63 mm²\n4. 6 mm² is less than 6.63 mm², so round up to 10 mm². 8.25 mm² uses the wrong k (115).","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b039","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"What is the maximum energy let-through (I²t) that a 1.5 mm² copper cpc with k = 115 can withstand?","options":["172.5 A²s","29,756 A²s","19,838 A²s","46,010 A²s"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Rearrange S = √(I²t)/k → I²t = k²S²\n2. k²S² = 115² x 1.5² = 13 225 x 2.25\n3. Result: 29,756 A²s\n4. 172.5 is just k x S (no squaring); 46,010 uses k = 143.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b040","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A circuit has Zs = 0.80 Ω at 230 V. The device curve shows it disconnects that fault current in 0.1 s. With k = 115, what is the minimum cpc csa?","options":["0.25 mm²","2.50 mm²","0.64 mm²","0.79 mm²"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Fault current If = Uo / Zs = 230 / 0.80 = 287.5 A\n2. I²t = 287.5² x 0.1 = 8266 A²s; √ = 90.92\n3. S = 90.92 / 115 = 0.79 mm²\n4. So a 1.0 mm² or larger cpc satisfies the adiabatic check.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b041","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"A 2.5 mm² copper conductor (k = 115) carries a fault current of 500 A. What is the maximum time it can carry this before exceeding its limiting temperature?","options":["0.57 s","0.51 s","0.17 s","0.33 s"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Rearrange S = √(I²t)/k → t = k²S² / I²\n2. k²S² = (115 x 2.5)² = 287.5² = 82656.25\n3. I² = 500² = 250 000\n4. t = 82656.25 / 250 000 = 0.33 s. The protective device must clear the fault faster than this.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b042","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"The value of k in the adiabatic equation depends on:","options":["The length of the circuit only","The protective device type only","The conductor material, its insulation, and the initial and final temperatures","The supply voltage and frequency"],"answer":2,"explain":"k is a factor that takes account of resistivity, heat capacity and the temperature limits of the conductor and its insulation. That is why a cpc inside a PVC cable (115) and a separate PVC cpc (143) have different values.","ref":"Reg 543.1.3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b043","topic":"Adiabatic","q":"Where is the disconnection time t in the adiabatic equation normally obtained from?","options":["Table 41.1 maximum disconnection times","It is always 0.4 s","It is always 5 s","The time/current characteristic of the protective device at the calculated fault current"],"answer":3,"explain":"You calculate the fault current (Uo / Zs) and read the actual operating time from the device's time/current curve (Appendix 3). Table 41.1 times are maximums for shock protection, not the actual clearance time.","ref":"Appendix 3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b044","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Using Cmin = 0.95 and Uo = 230 V, what is the maximum Zs for a Type B 32 A MCB (Ia = 5 x In)?","options":["1.44 Ω","0.68 Ω","6.83 Ω","1.37 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Ia = 5 x 32 = 160 A\n2. Formula: Zs = (Cmin x Uo) / Ia\n3. Zs = (0.95 x 230) / 160 = 218.5 / 160\n4. Result: Zs = 1.37 Ω. Leaving out Cmin gives 1.44 Ω; using 10In (Type C) gives 0.68 Ω.","ref":"Table 41.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b045","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A radial in 2.5/1.5 mm² cable (R1+R2 = 19.51 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is 25 m long. Ze = 0.35 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier for 70 °C, what is the expected Zs?","options":["0.84 Ω","0.59 Ω","0.94 Ω","1.01 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. R1+R2 at 20 °C = (19.51 x 25) / 1000 = 0.488 Ω\n2. At 70 °C: 0.488 x 1.2 = 0.585 Ω\n3. Zs = Ze + (R1+R2) = 0.35 + 0.585 = 0.935 Ω\n4. Result: 0.94 Ω. Forgetting the 1.2 gives 0.84 Ω; the 1.2 applies to the cable only, NOT to Ze.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b046","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"The tabulated maximum Zs for a B32 MCB is 1.37 Ω. A reading of 1.15 Ω is measured at ambient temperature. Applying the 80% rule of thumb, what is the limit and the verdict?","options":["1.71 Ω - the reading is acceptable","1.37 Ω - the reading is acceptable","1.10 Ω - the reading is too high and should be investigated","1.10 Ω - the reading is acceptable"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Rule of thumb: measured Zs should not exceed 0.8 x tabulated max (allows for conductor temperature rise under load)\n2. 0.8 x 1.37 = 1.096 Ω\n3. Compare: 1.15 Ω > 1.10 Ω\n4. The reading fails the rule of thumb, so it needs investigating even though it is below 1.37 Ω.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b047","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A B20 radial (max Zs 2.19 Ω) in 2.5/1.5 mm² cable (19.51 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is on a TN-S supply with Ze = 0.80 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier, what is the maximum length for shock protection?","options":["71.2 m","59.4 m","93.5 m","85.5 m"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Allowance for cable = Max Zs - Ze = 2.19 - 0.80 = 1.39 Ω (at 70 °C)\n2. Convert to 20 °C: 1.39 / 1.2 = 1.1583 Ω\n3. L = 1.1583 / 0.01951 Ω/m = 59.4 m\n4. Forgetting the 1.2 gives 71.2 m; ignoring Ze gives 93.5 m.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b048","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A B6 lighting circuit in 1.0/1.0 mm² cable (36.20 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is 30 m long. Ze = 0.80 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier, what is the expected Zs?","options":["1.89 Ω","2.10 Ω","1.30 Ω","2.26 Ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. R1+R2 at 20 °C = (36.20 x 30) / 1000 = 1.086 Ω\n2. At 70 °C: 1.086 x 1.2 = 1.303 Ω\n3. Zs = 0.80 + 1.303 = 2.103 Ω\n4. Result: 2.10 Ω - well within the B6 limit of 7.28 Ω.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b049","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A 40 A shower circuit in 6/2.5 mm² cable (R1+R2 = 10.49 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is 22 m long. Ze = 0.35 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier, what is the expected Zs?","options":["0.58 Ω","0.63 Ω","0.28 Ω","0.70 Ω"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. R1+R2 at 20 °C = (10.49 x 22) / 1000 = 0.2308 Ω\n2. At 70 °C: 0.2308 x 1.2 = 0.2769 Ω\n3. Zs = 0.35 + 0.2769 = 0.627 Ω\n4. Result: 0.63 Ω. Max for B40 = 0.95 x 230 / 200 = 1.09 Ω, so it complies.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b050","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Using Cmin = 0.95 and Uo = 230 V, what is the maximum Zs for a Type C 20 A MCB (Ia = 10 x In)?","options":["1.09 Ω","2.19 Ω","0.55 Ω","1.15 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Ia = 10 x 20 = 200 A\n2. Zs = (0.95 x 230) / 200 = 218.5 / 200\n3. Result: 1.09 Ω\n4. 2.19 Ω is the Type B value (5In); 0.55 Ω is Type D (20In).","ref":"Table 41.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b051","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Using Cmin = 0.95 and Uo = 230 V, what is the maximum Zs for a Type D 16 A MCB (Ia = 20 x In)?","options":["1.37 Ω","2.73 Ω","0.68 Ω","0.72 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Ia = 20 x 16 = 320 A\n2. Zs = (0.95 x 230) / 320 = 218.5 / 320\n3. Result: 0.68 Ω\n4. Type D needs a much lower Zs because it needs far more current to trip instantaneously.","ref":"Table 41.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b052","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"A B32 circuit has a measured Zs of 1.15 Ω. What earth fault current will flow (Uo = 230 V), and is it enough for instantaneous (magnetic) operation?","options":["200 A - no, it is below 320 A","264.5 A - yes, it exceeds 160 A","5.0 A - no, it is below 160 A","200 A - yes, it exceeds 160 A"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. If = Uo / Zs = 230 / 1.15 = 200 A\n2. B32 instantaneous trip current = 5 x 32 = 160 A\n3. 200 A > 160 A, so it will disconnect instantaneously\n4. 320 A would only apply to a Type C (10In).","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b053","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Supplementary bonding is being checked where the circuit is protected by a B16 MCB. Using R ≤ 50 V / Ia, with Ia taken as 5 x In, what is the maximum resistance allowed?","options":["3.125 Ω","0.313 Ω","0.625 Ω","1.600 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Ia = 5 x 16 = 80 A\n2. R ≤ 50 / Ia = 50 / 80\n3. Result: R ≤ 0.625 Ω\n4. 3.125 Ω wrongly uses In (16 A) instead of Ia; 0.313 Ω uses 10In (Type C); 1.600 Ω is the formula upside down.","ref":"Reg 415.2.2","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b054","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"How should Ze be measured at the origin of an installation?","options":["With all circuits switched on and bonding in place","Between line and neutral at the furthest socket","With the main switch on and the earthing conductor connected","With the installation isolated and the main earthing conductor disconnected from the MET"],"answer":3,"explain":"Disconnecting the earthing conductor removes parallel earth paths through the bonding, so you measure only the external loop. The installation must be isolated while the earthing conductor is off, and it must be reconnected before re-energising.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b055","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Which pair of maximum Ze values are commonly declared by DNOs for TN-C-S and TN-S supplies?","options":["TN-C-S 0.80 Ω; TN-S 0.35 Ω","TN-C-S 21 Ω; TN-S 0.80 Ω","TN-C-S 0.35 Ω; TN-S 0.80 Ω","TN-C-S 1.37 Ω; TN-S 2.19 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"0.35 Ω (TN-C-S) and 0.8 Ω (TN-S) are the typical maximum declared values. 21 Ω is the typical figure quoted for the DNO's contribution on a TT supply.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"EWA","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b056","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"Why is the factor Cmin (0.95) included in the maximum Zs calculation?","options":["To allow for the supply voltage being lower than nominal, which reduces fault current","To allow for the cable heating up to 70 °C","To convert AC to DC values","To allow for instrument inaccuracy only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cmin accounts for the minimum supply voltage. Lower voltage means lower fault current, so the maximum Zs is reduced to make sure the device still disconnects in time. Conductor temperature is dealt with separately (the 1.2 multiplier / 80% rule).","ref":"Table 41.3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b057","topic":"Earth fault loop & Zs","q":"In a TN system, what are the maximum disconnection times for a 230 V final circuit rated up to 32 A and for a distribution circuit?","options":["0.4 s for the final circuit; 5 s for the distribution circuit","0.2 s and 1 s","5 s and 0.4 s","0.4 s and 1 s"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 41.1 gives 0.4 s at 230 V for TN final circuits within its scope (up to 63 A with socket-outlets, or up to 32 A supplying only fixed equipment). Distribution circuits in a TN system are allowed 5 s. 0.2 s and 1 s are the corresponding TT values.","ref":"Table 41.1","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b058","topic":"Fault current","q":"Ze at the origin is measured as 0.25 Ω. What is the prospective earth fault current (Uo = 230 V)?","options":["0.0575 kA","1.60 kA","0.87 kA","0.92 kA"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Formula: PEFC = Uo / Ze\n2. PEFC = 230 / 0.25 = 920 A\n3. Result: 0.92 kA\n4. Using 400 V is for a line-line fault, not line-earth.","ref":"Fault current","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b059","topic":"Fault current","q":"The single-phase prospective short-circuit current at a three-phase board is 2.4 kA. Using the standard approximation, what is the three-phase PSCC?","options":["4.16 kA","4.8 kA","1.2 kA","7.2 kA"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Approximation: 3-phase PSCC ≈ 2 x single-phase PSCC\n2. 2 x 2.4 kA\n3. Result: 4.8 kA\n4. This simple doubling is the accepted rule of thumb; multiplying by √3 or 3 is incorrect for this method.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b060","topic":"Fault current","q":"The line-neutral loop impedance at the origin measures 0.16 Ω. What is the PSCC (Uo = 230 V)?","options":["2.50 kA","0.037 kA","1.44 kA","1.37 kA"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Formula: PSCC = Uo / Z(L-N)\n2. PSCC = 230 / 0.16 = 1437.5 A\n3. Result: 1.44 kA\n4. Record this on the schedule; the higher of PSCC and PEFC is the PFC.","ref":"Fault current","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b061","topic":"Fault current","q":"Supply line-neutral impedance is 0.25 Ω. A radial in 2.5/2.5 mm² cable (R1+Rn = 14.82 mΩ/m at 20 °C) is 20 m long. What is the maximum prospective short-circuit current at the end of the circuit? (Use 20 °C values for maximum fault current.)","options":["920 A","421 A","380 A","776 A"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. R1+Rn = (14.82 x 20) / 1000 = 0.2964 Ω\n2. Total Z = 0.25 + 0.2964 = 0.5464 Ω\n3. I = 230 / 0.5464 = 421 A\n4. For MAXIMUM fault current the conductors are taken cold (no 1.2 multiplier). Applying 1.2 gives the lower figure of 380 A.","ref":"Fault current","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b062","topic":"Fault current","q":"What value of prospective fault current should be recorded at the origin on test certificates?","options":["The lower of PSCC and PEFC","The average of PSCC and PEFC","Always the earth fault current","The greater of the prospective short-circuit current and the prospective earth fault current"],"answer":3,"explain":"The PFC recorded is the higher of the two, because the protective devices must have a breaking capacity that can cope with the worst-case fault.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b063","topic":"Fault current","q":"A domestic consumer unit to BS EN 61439-3 has a conditional short-circuit rating of 16 kA, but the MCBs fitted are marked 6000. The PFC is 3.2 kA. What is correct?","options":["The MCBs must be replaced with 10 kA devices","The PFC must be below 6 A","The consumer unit is only suitable up to 1.6 kA","The arrangement is acceptable - PFC is below both values"],"answer":3,"explain":"The '6000' marking is the MCB's short-circuit capacity (6 kA). 3.2 kA is within that and within the assembly's conditional rating, so the devices are adequate.","ref":"Fault current","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b064","topic":"TT systems","q":"For a TT installation protected by a 30 mA RCD, what is the maximum earth electrode resistance RA allowed by RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V?","options":["1667 Ω","1.67 Ω","200 Ω","150 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: RA ≤ 50 / IΔn\n2. IΔn = 30 mA = 0.03 A\n3. RA ≤ 50 / 0.03 = 1666.7 Ω\n4. Forgetting to convert mA to A gives 1.67 Ω. In practice aim much lower - values over 200 Ω may not be stable.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b065","topic":"TT systems","q":"For a TT installation using a 100 mA RCD, what is the maximum RA from RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V?","options":["500 Ω","5000 Ω","0.5 Ω","1667 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. RA ≤ 50 / IΔn\n2. IΔn = 100 mA = 0.1 A\n3. RA ≤ 50 / 0.1 = 500 Ω\n4. 1667 Ω is the 30 mA figure.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b066","topic":"TT systems","q":"An upfront time-delayed 300 mA RCD protects a TT installation. What is the maximum RA from RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V?","options":["15 Ω","500 Ω","167 Ω","1667 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. RA ≤ 50 / IΔn\n2. IΔn = 300 mA = 0.3 A\n3. RA ≤ 50 / 0.3 = 166.7 Ω (≈ 167 Ω)\n4. The larger the RCD rating, the lower the electrode resistance must be.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b067","topic":"TT systems","q":"A TT electrode measures RA = 145 Ω and the installation is protected by a 100 mA RCD. What is the touch voltage figure RA x IΔn, and does it comply?","options":["14.5 V - does not comply","1450 V - does not comply","14.5 V - complies","145 V - does not comply"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. RA x IΔn = 145 x 0.1\n2. Result: 14.5 V\n3. 14.5 V ≤ 50 V, so it complies\n4. 145 Ω is also below 200 Ω, so the reading is likely to be stable.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b068","topic":"TT systems","q":"An installation with a 300 mA S-type RCD has RA = 88 Ω. What is RA x IΔn and does it comply?","options":["26.4 V - does not comply","26400 V - does not comply","26.4 V - complies","293.3 V - does not comply"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. RA x IΔn = 88 x 0.3\n2. Result: 26.4 V\n3. 26.4 V ≤ 50 V, so it complies\n4. Dividing by IΔn (293.3) is the wrong way round.","ref":"Reg 411.5.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b069","topic":"TT systems","q":"A TT installation has RA = 60 Ω (ignore all other impedances). If a B32 MCB alone were relied on for fault protection, what earth fault current would flow and would it trip instantaneously?","options":["3.83 A - yes, above 32 A","13800 A - yes, above 160 A","3.83 A - yes, above 0.03 A","3.83 A - no, far below the 160 A needed"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. If = Uo / RA = 230 / 60 = 3.83 A\n2. Instantaneous trip for B32 = 5 x 32 = 160 A\n3. 3.83 A is far too small - the MCB would not operate in time (it may not operate at all)\n4. This is why TT installations rely on RCDs for fault protection.","ref":"TT systems","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b070","topic":"TT systems","q":"According to GN3, what should be borne in mind if a TT earth electrode resistance exceeds 200 Ω?","options":["It is automatically non-compliant with a 30 mA RCD","It means the electrode is too deep","It must be recorded as C1","The value may not be stable (e.g. soil drying or freezing) and should be improved"],"answer":3,"explain":"Although RA x IΔn ≤ 50 V would allow up to 1667 Ω with a 30 mA RCD, GN3 notes that readings above 200 Ω may not be stable over time due to changing ground conditions.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b071","topic":"TT systems","q":"In a TT system, what is the maximum disconnection time for a 230 V final circuit rated up to 32 A?","options":["0.2 s","0.4 s","1 s","5 s"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 41.1 gives 0.2 s at 230 V for TT final circuits within its scope (0.4 s is the TN value). Distribution circuits in TT systems are allowed 1 s.","ref":"Table 41.1","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b072","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"Ring final end-to-end readings are r1 = 0.60 Ω, rn = 0.60 Ω, r2 = 1.00 Ω. After cross-connecting line and neutral, what reading would you expect at each socket (L-N)?","options":["0.30 Ω","1.20 Ω","0.60 Ω","0.15 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. Formula: L-N reading ≈ (r1 + rn) / 4\n2. (0.60 + 0.60) / 4 = 1.20 / 4\n3. Result: 0.30 Ω at every socket (roughly the same all round)\n4. 1.20 Ω forgets to divide by 4.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b073","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"Ring end-to-end readings are r1 = 0.60 Ω and r2 = 1.00 Ω. With line and cpc cross-connected, what R1+R2 would you expect at each socket?","options":["1.60 Ω","0.80 Ω","0.40 Ω","0.30 Ω"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Formula: R1+R2 ≈ (r1 + r2) / 4\n2. (0.60 + 1.00) / 4 = 1.60 / 4\n3. Result: 0.40 Ω\n4. This value is the R1+R2 for each socket and is used with Ze to find Zs. The highest reading is recorded.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b074","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"A ring final in 2.5/1.5 mm² cable has a total length of 70 m. Given 2.5 mm² = 7.41 mΩ/m and 1.5 mm² = 12.10 mΩ/m (20 °C), what R1+R2 would you expect at each socket after cross-connection?","options":["1.37 Ω","0.68 Ω","0.41 Ω","0.34 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. r1 = 7.41 x 70 / 1000 = 0.519 Ω\n2. r2 = 12.10 x 70 / 1000 = 0.847 Ω\n3. R1+R2 = (r1 + r2) / 4 = (0.519 + 0.847) / 4 = 1.366 / 4\n4. Result: 0.34 Ω (for a test at about 20 °C, so no 1.2 multiplier).","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b075","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"A ring's end-to-end line reading r1 = 0.64 Ω. After cross-connection, the R1+R2 readings at the sockets are all about 0.42 Ω. What is the implied end-to-end cpc reading r2?","options":["1.04 Ω","1.68 Ω","0.26 Ω","0.20 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. R1+R2 = (r1 + r2) / 4 → r2 = 4 x (R1+R2) - r1\n2. r2 = 4 x 0.42 - 0.64 = 1.68 - 0.64\n3. Result: r2 = 1.04 Ω\n4. That is about 1.6 x r1, which is consistent with a 2.5/1.5 cable.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b076","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"End-to-end values for a ring, calculated at 20 °C, are r1 = 0.50 Ω and r2 = 0.84 Ω. Ze = 0.35 Ω. Using the 1.2 multiplier, what is the expected Zs at the sockets?","options":["0.75 Ω","0.68 Ω","1.96 Ω","1.15 Ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. R1+R2 = (0.50 + 0.84) / 4 = 0.335 Ω\n2. At 70 °C: 0.335 x 1.2 = 0.402 Ω\n3. Zs = 0.35 + 0.402 = 0.752 Ω\n4. Result: 0.75 Ω, well inside 1.37 Ω for a B32. Forgetting the ÷4 gives a wildly high answer.","ref":"Earth fault loop & Zs","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b077","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"After cross-connecting a ring, every socket reads R1+R2 ≈ 0.30 Ω except one, which reads 0.38 Ω. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["The ring has an interconnection","The ring is broken in the neutral","That socket is on an unfused spur adding about 0.08 Ω","The ends were not cross-connected correctly"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Readings on a correctly cross-connected ring should be substantially the same\n2. Extra = 0.38 - 0.30 = 0.08 Ω at one point only\n3. A single higher reading points to a spur (the extra is the spur's R1+R2) or a poor termination at that socket\n4. Incorrect cross-connection shows a steady rise towards the middle, not one outlier.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b078","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"Ring end-to-end readings: r1 = 0.60 Ω, rn = 0.85 Ω, r2 = 1.00 Ω. What does this indicate?","options":["Everything is normal","An interconnection in the cpc","The cable is 2.5/1.5 so this is expected","A likely high-resistance joint or loose connection in the neutral conductor"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. r1 and rn are the same csa so should be within 0.05 Ω of each other\n2. Difference = 0.85 - 0.60 = 0.25 Ω, far above 0.05 Ω\n3. That points to a loose/high-resistance neutral termination somewhere on the ring\n4. r2 being higher than r1 is expected because the cpc is smaller.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b079","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"When testing a ring, the L-N readings at the sockets rise steadily towards the middle of the ring and then fall again. What is the most likely cause?","options":["There is a spur on the midpoint socket","The cpc is broken","The ends of the ring were not cross-connected correctly (same ends joined)","The ring is healthy"],"answer":2,"explain":"When correctly cross-connected (line of one end to neutral of the other), every socket reads about the same. A reading that peaks mid-ring shows the ends have been joined like-to-like rather than crossed.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b080","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"What is the maximum difference GN3 expects between the end-to-end readings of r1 and rn on a ring of the same csa?","options":["0.5 Ω","0.005 Ω","0.2 Ω","0.05 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"Line and neutral are the same size and length, so their end-to-end resistances should be within 0.05 Ω of each other. A bigger difference suggests a poor connection.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b081","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"During step 1 of a ring test, the end-to-end reading of r1 is open circuit (OL) but rn and r2 read normally. What does this indicate?","options":["An interconnection in the ring","A short circuit L-N","A break in the line conductor somewhere in the ring","The ring is actually a radial with a spur"],"answer":2,"explain":"An open-circuit end-to-end reading means the conductor path is not continuous. Rn and r2 reading normally narrows it to the line conductor - check each socket's line terminations.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b082","topic":"Ring final testing","q":"The end-to-end readings on a 90 m ring are much LOWER than expected for its length, and the socket readings after cross-connection vary. What is the most likely fault?","options":["An interconnection (bridge) forming a figure-of-eight in the ring","A break in the cpc","A high-resistance joint","Incorrect polarity at one socket"],"answer":0,"explain":"A bridge between two points of a ring shortens the conductor path, so end-to-end readings are lower than the cable length suggests and socket readings are uneven. A break or high-resistance joint would give higher or open readings.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b083","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"Four circuits are tested separately at a board with IR values of 200 MΩ, 100 MΩ, 50 MΩ and 25 MΩ. What would the combined reading be if tested together?","options":["375.00 MΩ","93.75 MΩ","25.00 MΩ","13.33 MΩ"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. IR paths are in parallel: 1/RT = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3 + 1/R4\n2. 1/RT = 0.005 + 0.010 + 0.020 + 0.040 = 0.075\n3. RT = 1 / 0.075 = 13.33 MΩ\n4. The combined value is always LOWER than the lowest individual reading. 375 MΩ adds them as if in series.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b084","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"Two circuits have insulation resistances of 3 MΩ and 6 MΩ. What is their combined insulation resistance?","options":["9.00 MΩ","4.50 MΩ","2.00 MΩ","0.50 MΩ"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Product over sum: RT = (3 x 6) / (3 + 6) = 18 / 9\n2. Result: 2.00 MΩ\n3. Check: 1/3 + 1/6 = 0.5; 1/0.5 = 2 MΩ\n4. 0.5 is 1/RT - you must take the reciprocal.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b085","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"Ten identical circuits each have an insulation resistance of 20 MΩ. What is the combined value when tested together at the main switch?","options":["200.00 MΩ","2.00 MΩ","20.00 MΩ","0.50 MΩ"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. For n identical parallel paths: RT = R / n\n2. RT = 20 / 10\n3. Result: 2.00 MΩ\n4. This is why a whole-installation reading can look low even when every circuit is healthy.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b086","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"A distribution board's overall IR reading is 1.2 MΩ. One circuit on its own reads 2 MΩ. What is the combined IR of all the other circuits?","options":["3.00 MΩ","0.80 MΩ","0.75 MΩ","3.20 MΩ"],"answer":0,"explain":"1. 1/RT = 1/R1 + 1/Rrest → 1/Rrest = 1/RT - 1/R1\n2. 1/Rrest = 1/1.2 - 1/2 = 0.8333 - 0.5 = 0.3333\n3. Rrest = 1 / 0.3333 = 3.00 MΩ\n4. Simple subtraction (2 - 1.2 = 0.8) does not work with parallel resistances.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b087","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"What test voltage and minimum insulation resistance apply to a 230 V final circuit (Table 64)?","options":["250 V d.c.; 0.5 MΩ","1000 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","500 V d.c.; 0.5 MΩ","500 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ"],"answer":3,"explain":"For circuits up to and including 500 V (other than SELV/PELV) the test is at 500 V d.c. and the minimum is 1.0 MΩ.","ref":"Table 64","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b088","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"What test voltage and minimum insulation resistance apply to a SELV or PELV circuit?","options":["250 V d.c.; 0.5 MΩ","500 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","250 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","100 V d.c.; 0.25 MΩ"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 64 requires 250 V d.c. with a minimum of 0.5 MΩ for SELV and PELV circuits.","ref":"Table 64","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b089","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"What test voltage and minimum insulation resistance apply to a circuit with a nominal voltage above 500 V (e.g. 690 V)?","options":["500 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","1000 V d.c.; 1.0 MΩ","1000 V d.c.; 2.0 MΩ","2500 V d.c.; 0.5 MΩ"],"answer":1,"explain":"Table 64: circuits above 500 V are tested at 1000 V d.c. with a minimum of 1.0 MΩ.","ref":"Table 64","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b090","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"An SPD in a circuit cannot practicably be disconnected for the insulation resistance test. What does BS 7671 allow?","options":["Omit the test on that circuit","Test at 1000 V d.c. to overcome the SPD","Test that circuit at 250 V d.c., with a minimum of 1 MΩ","Test at 250 V d.c. with a minimum of 0.5 MΩ"],"answer":2,"explain":"Where SPDs or other equipment likely to influence the test cannot be disconnected, the test voltage may be reduced to 250 V d.c., but the insulation resistance must still be at least 1 MΩ.","ref":"Reg 643.3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b091","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"An individual circuit on an EIC gives an IR reading of 1.6 MΩ at 500 V. It meets the Table 64 minimum. What does GN3 advise?","options":["Nothing - it passes","It should be investigated, as readings under 2 MΩ may indicate a latent defect","It must be retested at 1000 V","It must be coded C1"],"answer":1,"explain":"Although 1.6 MΩ exceeds 1 MΩ, GN3 notes that a reading below 2 MΩ on an individual circuit can indicate a latent defect, and each circuit should be tested separately to find it.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b092","topic":"Insulation resistance","q":"Before an insulation resistance test on a circuit, why should lamps and electronic equipment be removed or disconnected?","options":["They increase the IR reading to infinity","They may be damaged by the test voltage and can give false low readings","The test is only valid with loads connected","It is only needed for SELV circuits"],"answer":1,"explain":"The d.c. test voltage can damage electronics, and connected loads give a path between L and N that produces a misleadingly low reading.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b093","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A general-purpose 30 mA RCD to BS EN 61008 is tested at its rated residual current (IΔn). What is the maximum permitted operating time?","options":["40 ms","200 ms","500 ms","300 ms"],"answer":3,"explain":"At 1 x IΔn a general (non-delay) RCD to BS EN 61008/61009 must trip within 300 ms.","ref":"RCD & polarity testing","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b094","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A 30 mA RCD provides additional protection. What test current is used for the 5 x IΔn test and what is the maximum trip time?","options":["150 mA; 300 ms","60 mA; 40 ms","30 mA; 40 ms","150 mA; 40 ms"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. Test current = 5 x IΔn = 5 x 30 mA = 150 mA\n2. Required operating time for additional protection = 40 ms max\n3. 300 ms is the requirement at 1 x IΔn.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b095","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A 30 mA RCD providing additional protection gives: 1 x IΔn = 280 ms; 5 x IΔn = 46 ms. What is the verdict?","options":["Pass - both are under 300 ms","Fail - the 1 x IΔn time exceeds 200 ms","Pass - 46 ms is under 50 ms","Fail - the 5 x IΔn time exceeds 40 ms"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. 1 x IΔn: 280 ms ≤ 300 ms - OK\n2. 5 x IΔn: 46 ms > 40 ms - FAIL\n3. The device does not provide additional protection and should be investigated/replaced.","ref":"RCD & polarity testing","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b096","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A time-delayed (S-type) RCD is tested at 1 x IΔn. What operating time range is acceptable?","options":["130 ms to 500 ms","0 to 40 ms","0 to 300 ms","300 ms to 1 s"],"answer":0,"explain":"S-type RCDs are deliberately delayed for discrimination with downstream RCDs; at IΔn they must operate no sooner than 130 ms and no later than 500 ms.","ref":"RCD & polarity testing","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b097","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"Why are RCD trip-time tests carried out at both 0° and 180°?","options":["To test both line and neutral poles separately","Operating time can differ depending on the point on the waveform; the longer time is recorded","To check the test button","Because the 180° test is for three-phase RCDs only"],"answer":1,"explain":"An RCD may respond differently depending on whether the test starts on the positive or negative half-cycle. Test at both and record the longer (worst) time.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b098","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"What does pressing an RCD's integral test button prove?","options":["That the trip time is within 40 ms","That the earth fault loop impedance is acceptable","That polarity is correct","That the mechanical tripping mechanism works - not the trip time or sensitivity"],"answer":3,"explain":"The test button creates an imbalance to check the mechanism operates. It does not verify operating times, which need an RCD tester.","ref":"RCD & polarity testing","level":"EWA","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b099","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"Which requirement is checked by the polarity test for single-pole devices?","options":["All switches are connected in the neutral","The cpc is connected to the neutral terminal","RCDs trip within 40 ms","Every fuse and single-pole control/protective device is connected in the line conductor only"],"answer":3,"explain":"Polarity testing confirms single-pole devices are in the line conductor so that switching off isolates the line from the equipment.","ref":"Reg 643.6","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b100","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"For an Edison-screw lampholder (other than E14 and E27 types to BS EN 60238), which conductor must connect to the centre contact?","options":["The neutral conductor","The protective conductor","The line conductor","Either, as it is a.c."],"answer":2,"explain":"The centre contact must be connected to line so the screw shell, which may be touched, is at neutral potential.","ref":"Reg 643.6","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b101","topic":"RCD & polarity testing","q":"A common dead-test method for polarity on a radial circuit is to:","options":["Measure IR between L and N with lamps in","Link line to cpc at the board and measure between line and cpc at each point with a low-resistance ohmmeter","Use an RCD tester at each socket","Measure voltage between L and N at each socket"],"answer":1,"explain":"With L linked to cpc at the origin, a low reading between the line terminal and cpc at each point confirms polarity and gives R1+R2 at the same time. Remove the link after testing.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b102","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"For a new installation, which test is normally carried out FIRST in the GN3 sequence?","options":["Earth fault loop impedance","RCD operation","Insulation resistance","Continuity of protective conductors (including bonding)"],"answer":3,"explain":"Continuity of protective conductors comes first so that a sound earth path is proven before any other test (especially before energising).","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b103","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Which is the correct order for these tests on a new installation?","options":["Insulation resistance → continuity → RCD → polarity → Zs","Zs → RCD → continuity → IR → polarity","Continuity → insulation resistance → polarity → earth fault loop impedance → RCD","Polarity → RCD → Zs → IR → continuity"],"answer":2,"explain":"Dead tests (continuity, insulation resistance, polarity) come before live tests (Zs, PFC, RCD operation), so faults are found before the installation is energised.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b104","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Why must dead tests be completed before live tests?","options":["Because live tests are quicker","Because instruments can only test live after calibration","It does not matter which order","To prove the installation is safe to energise (no shorts, sound cpc, correct polarity) before connecting the supply"],"answer":3,"explain":"Energising an installation with a broken cpc, a short circuit or reversed polarity could be dangerous. The dead tests find those faults first.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b105","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"A low-resistance ohmmeter used for continuity testing should have which characteristics (BS EN 61557-4)?","options":["Test current of 1 mA at 500 V","Test current of 30 mA at 230 V","Test current of at least 200 mA and open-circuit voltage of 4 V to 24 V","Test current of 10 A at 400 V"],"answer":2,"explain":"Continuity testers must deliver at least 200 mA with an open-circuit voltage between 4 V and 24 V. 1 mA at the test voltage is the requirement for insulation testers.","ref":"BS EN 61557-4","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b106","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"An electrician forgets to null the test leads. The leads measure 0.12 Ω and the R1+R2 reading taken with them is 0.61 Ω. What is the true R1+R2?","options":["0.73 Ω","0.61 Ω","0.12 Ω","0.49 Ω"],"answer":3,"explain":"1. True value = instrument reading - lead resistance\n2. 0.61 - 0.12\n3. Result: 0.49 Ω\n4. Always null the leads (or subtract them) before recording continuity values.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b107","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Using the fall-of-potential (three-terminal) method, where is the potential spike normally placed relative to the current spike?","options":["At the electrode under test","About 62% of the distance from the electrode under test to the current spike","At 10% of the distance","Beyond the current spike"],"answer":1,"explain":"The potential spike is placed at about 62% of the way to the current spike, and readings are repeated with it moved either side to confirm they are consistent.","ref":"GN3","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b108","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"What does the 'wander lead' (R2) method measure?","options":["R1+R2 at each point","The resistance of the protective conductor (R2) only, from the MET to the point being tested","The earth electrode resistance","Line to neutral loop impedance"],"answer":1,"explain":"One end of a long lead goes to the MET, the other to the tester; the other test lead touches the cpc at each point. It is also used for bonding conductors. R1+R2 needs the line linked to cpc instead.","ref":"GN3","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b109","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Which standard covers insulation resistance testers?","options":["BS EN 61557-2","BS EN 61557-4","BS EN 61557-6","BS EN 61008"],"answer":0,"explain":"BS EN 61557-2 covers insulation resistance testers, -3 loop impedance, -4 continuity and -6 RCD testers.","ref":"Test sequence & instruments","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b110","topic":"Test sequence & instruments","q":"Before starting dead testing, what is the correct way to confirm a circuit is dead?","options":["Use a neon screwdriver","Use an approved voltage indicator (to GS38), proved on a known source before and after the test","Switch off and trust the MCB","Use the insulation tester on its voltage range without proving it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Safe isolation requires an approved voltage indicator proved on a proving unit or known live source before and after checking the circuit is dead.","ref":"GS38","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b111","topic":"EICR coding","q":"On an EICR, what does classification code C1 mean?","options":["Potentially dangerous - urgent remedial action required","Danger present - risk of injury, immediate remedial action required","Improvement recommended","Further investigation required without delay"],"answer":1,"explain":"C1 is for danger that exists now; the person ordering the work should be told immediately and the danger made safe where possible.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b112","topic":"EICR coding","q":"What does classification code C2 mean?","options":["Danger present - immediate action required","Potentially dangerous - urgent remedial action required","Improvement recommended","No action required"],"answer":1,"explain":"C2 is for a defect that is not immediately dangerous but would become a danger if a further fault or event occurred.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b113","topic":"EICR coding","q":"An EICR contains only C3 observations. What is the overall assessment?","options":["Unsatisfactory","Satisfactory","Satisfactory only if the C3s are fixed within 28 days","It cannot be assessed"],"answer":1,"explain":"C3 means improvement recommended; it does not by itself make the installation unsatisfactory.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b114","topic":"EICR coding","q":"Which observations make the overall condition of an installation UNSATISFACTORY?","options":["Any C1, C2 or FI","Any C3","Only C1","Three or more C3s"],"answer":0,"explain":"The presence of any C1, C2 or FI code means the overall assessment must be unsatisfactory.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b115","topic":"EICR coding","q":"When should code FI be used?","options":["For any minor departure from the current edition","When the client refuses access to one room","Whenever a test result is borderline","When an observation needs further investigation that cannot be done during the inspection, but may reveal a dangerous or potentially dangerous condition"],"answer":3,"explain":"FI means further investigation required without delay. It flags a possible C1/C2 issue whose cause could not be determined within the extent of the inspection.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b116","topic":"EICR coding","q":"Blanks are missing from a consumer unit, leaving live busbars accessible to touch. What code is appropriate?","options":["C2","C1","C3","FI"],"answer":1,"explain":"Accessible live parts are an immediate danger. Code C1 and make safe (e.g. fit blanks) with the client's agreement before leaving.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b117","topic":"EICR coding","q":"There is no main protective bonding to the incoming metallic water service. What code is appropriate?","options":["C1","C3","C2","No code"],"answer":2,"explain":"Missing main bonding is potentially dangerous (it would become dangerous in the event of a fault), so C2 applies.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b118","topic":"EICR coding","q":"A circuit's measured Zs exceeds the maximum for its overcurrent device and there is no RCD protection. What code is appropriate?","options":["C3","No code","C2","FI"],"answer":2,"explain":"Fault protection is not assured - the device may not disconnect in the required time under fault conditions. This is potentially dangerous, so C2.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b119","topic":"EICR coding","q":"An installation was installed to an earlier edition of BS 7671 and does not comply with some current requirements, but is otherwise safe. How should this be treated?","options":["Non-compliance with the current edition alone does not mean it is unsafe; code only on the basis of safety","Every non-compliance must be coded C2","The installation must be marked unsatisfactory","Every non-compliance must be coded FI"],"answer":0,"explain":"An EICR assesses safety for continued use. A departure from the current edition is not automatically dangerous; use judgement and code according to the actual risk.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b120","topic":"EICR coding","q":"How should limitations on an EICR be dealt with?","options":["They do not need to be recorded","Agreed with the client before starting where possible, and recorded on the report with reasons","They are only recorded if the result is unsatisfactory","They must be coded C3"],"answer":1,"explain":"The extent of the inspection and any agreed or operational limitations (e.g. no access, sampling percentage) must be recorded so the reader knows what was and was not checked.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b121","topic":"EICR coding","q":"Which documents must accompany an EICR for it to be complete?","options":["An EIC and a minor works certificate","Only a photograph of the consumer unit","The original installation's EIC","A schedule of inspections and a schedule of test results"],"answer":3,"explain":"An EICR without its schedule of inspections and schedule of test results is incomplete.","ref":"EICR coding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b122","topic":"Fault finding","q":"A loose terminal has a resistance of 0.5 Ω and carries 20 A. How much heat (power) is produced at the joint?","options":["10 W","40 W","200 W","800 W"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. Formula: P = I²R\n2. P = 20² x 0.5 = 400 x 0.5\n3. Result: 200 W - enough to scorch accessories and cause a fire\n4. 10 W is I x R (the volt drop).","ref":"Fault finding","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b123","topic":"Fault finding","q":"On a radial socket circuit, the expected R1+R2 at the last socket is 0.49 Ω, but the reading at the second socket is already 0.95 Ω while the first reads normally. What does this suggest?","options":["The circuit is healthy","An interconnection between two circuits","A high-resistance joint (at least 0.46 Ω) between the first and second sockets","A short circuit between line and cpc"],"answer":2,"explain":"1. On a radial, R1+R2 rises steadily along the run, so the second socket should read LESS than the 0.49 Ω expected at the last socket\n2. It reads 0.95 Ω, so there is at least 0.95 - 0.49 = 0.46 Ω of extra resistance (the true excess is a little more, as socket 2's normal value is below 0.49 Ω)\n3. Because the first socket reads normally, the added resistance lies between socket 1 and socket 2 - check those terminations and any joints.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b124","topic":"Fault finding","q":"Four computers each have 2.5 mA of protective conductor current and share one 30 mA RCD. Using the guideline that accumulated protective conductor current should not exceed 30% of IΔn, is this acceptable?","options":["Yes - 10 mA is below 30 mA","No - 10 mA exceeds the 9 mA limit","Yes - 2.5 mA is below 9 mA","No - 10 mA exceeds the 3 mA limit"],"answer":1,"explain":"1. Total leakage = 4 x 2.5 = 10 mA\n2. Limit = 30% x 30 mA = 9 mA\n3. 10 mA > 9 mA, so nuisance tripping is likely - split the loads across RCDs/RCBOs.","ref":"Reg 531.3.2","level":"2391","calc":true,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b125","topic":"Fault finding","q":"In a split-load board, an RCD trips whenever a lighting circuit on the OTHER RCD is switched on. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A borrowed neutral - the lighting circuit's neutral returns via a circuit on the other RCD","A high Ze","A faulty MCB on the lighting circuit","A broken cpc"],"answer":0,"explain":"Current goes out through one RCD and returns through another, so both see an imbalance and trip. Borrowed neutrals also leave 'isolated' circuits with a live neutral.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b126","topic":"Fault finding","q":"How could you confirm a suspected borrowed neutral between two circuits with the installation isolated?","options":["Test IR between line and cpc","Carry out an RCD test at 5 x IΔn","Measure Ze at the origin","Disconnect both neutrals from the neutral bar and test continuity between them"],"answer":3,"explain":"Once removed from the bar, the two neutrals should be separate. Continuity between them shows they are connected somewhere in the installation.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b127","topic":"Fault finding","q":"An RCD protecting several circuits trips as soon as a particular load is switched on, but MCBs do not operate. IR between neutral and earth on that circuit is 0.02 MΩ. What is the fault?","options":["A line-to-neutral short circuit","An open-circuit neutral","An overload","A neutral-to-earth fault"],"answer":3,"explain":"A low N-E insulation resistance lets load current return through the cpc instead of the neutral. The RCD sees the imbalance and trips, while the MCB sees no overcurrent.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b128","topic":"Fault finding","q":"An insulation resistance test between line and neutral on a radial reads 0.00 MΩ with all loads disconnected. What does this indicate?","options":["An open circuit in the line","A healthy circuit","A broken cpc","A short circuit between line and neutral"],"answer":3,"explain":"A near-zero IR between live conductors with loads removed means the conductors are in contact - e.g. a damaged cable or a trapped conductor in a box.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b129","topic":"Fault finding","q":"A continuity test of R1+R2 at a lighting point reads open circuit (OL), but the light works normally. What is the most likely fault?","options":["A short circuit L-N","Incorrect polarity","A high Ze","An open circuit in the cpc (e.g. a disconnected earth at a switch or ceiling rose)"],"answer":3,"explain":"The line conductor is clearly continuous because the light works, so an OL R1+R2 reading points to a broken or disconnected cpc - a dangerous fault.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b130","topic":"Fault finding","q":"A low IR reading is found on a long radial with several junction boxes. What is the efficient way to find the fault?","options":["Replace the whole circuit","Test at 1000 V to burn out the fault","Carry out an RCD test at each outlet","Split the circuit at a midpoint and test each half, then repeat on the faulty half"],"answer":3,"explain":"The half-split method quickly narrows down the faulty section, reducing the number of accessories that need to be opened up.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"AM2E","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"},{"id":"b131","topic":"Fault finding","q":"On a TN-C-S (PME) supply, what is the main danger if the supply PEN conductor becomes open circuit?","options":["Exposed and extraneous-conductive-parts connected to the MET can rise to a dangerous voltage","The RCD will trip and make everything safe","Only the lights will dim","The MCBs will trip on overload"],"answer":0,"explain":"The PEN carries both neutral and protective functions. If it breaks, load current can drive the earthed metalwork towards line voltage - an RCD will not necessarily detect this.","ref":"Fault finding","level":"2391","calc":false,"area":"Calcs & testing"}];
window.BF_AM2E = {"updated":"2026-09","note":"Original checklist written for an experienced electrician (2391 holder) sitting AM2E / AM2E v1 at the end of the EWA route. Section codes follow NET's AM2E page (A1, A2-A6, B, C, D, E); the older all-versions Pre-Assessment Manual letters them A-F instead. Timings and knowledge-test format differ between NET sources and between AM2E and AM2E v1, so confirm the version you are registered on and its timings with NET or your centre.","sources":[{"title":"NET - AM2E Assessment for Experienced Workers (section list, timings, versions, fees, re-sit policy, booking evidence)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/am2e/"},{"title":"NET - AM2E V1.1 Full Candidate Checklist, July 2024 (hosted by South East AM2 Centres)","url":"https://southeastam2centres.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/2025/08/NET-AM2E-V1.1-Full-Candidate-Checklist-July-24.pdf"},{"title":"NET - Pre-Assessment Manual: AM2 all versions (v2023.01) (tasks per section, what is provided, tools, common failure indicators, results process)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/NET-AM2-Pre-Assessment-Manual-v2023.01.pdf"},{"title":"NET - Pre-Assessment Manual index page","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/pre-assessment-manual/"},{"title":"NET - FAQs (objective marking, re-sit timing, 2-year validity, permitted tools and publications)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/faq/"},{"title":"NET - AM2 Candidate Self-Assessment Checklist, May 2025 (cable types, comparison only)","url":"https://www.netservices.org.uk/wp-content/uploads/2024/07/NET-AM2-Candidate-Checklist-2505-WE2.pdf"}],"sections":[{"id":"s1","title":"A1 - Safe isolation and risk assessment","time":"45 min (NET AM2E page and V1.1 checklist); the manual adds about 10 min for reading and preparation","what":"Safe isolation of a distribution board in the correct order, a full set of voltage-indicator checks across every conductor combination, and a written risk assessment for the work. Most people treat this as the section where one missed step fails you. The marking is yes/no against set criteria.","items":["Can I do a written risk assessment for the installation task (hazards, who is at risk, controls, residual risk) without a template to copy from?","Can I identify the correct point of isolation from the drawings and labelling before touching anything?","Can I prove my approved voltage indicator (GS38: fused leads, shrouded probes, minimal exposed tip) on a proving unit or known live source before testing?","Can I carry out the full 10-point test on a TP&N board (L1-L2, L2-L3, L3-L1, each line to N, each line to E, N-E) without losing my place?","Do I test on the correct side of the isolator, with the switch in the correct position, and check that nothing is backfed?","Can I secure the isolation with a lock and keep the key on me, then fit a warning notice in the right place every time?","Do I re-prove the voltage indicator on the proving unit AFTER the dead test, every time, without being prompted?","Can I say out loud why each step is there? (The assessor may ask, and saying it keeps you in sequence.)","Do I check for separate or alternative supplies and stored energy before calling the circuit safe?","Can I get through the whole sequence calmly inside the time, with nothing left out?"],"fails":["Leaving out one or more of the 10 test combinations","Not proving the voltage indicator before AND after","Isolation not locked, key left in or lying around, or no warning notice","Testing with the isolator in the wrong position or on the wrong side","A thin or generic risk assessment that doesn't match the actual task"]},{"id":"s2","title":"A2-A6 - Composite installation","time":"10 h per NET AM2E page (and the manual's AM2S/E figure), but 10.5 h per AM2E V1.1 checklist - check with NET for your version. The manual adds about 15 min for preparation.","what":"A realistic installation built exactly to a specification and drawings. It includes a three-phase board and sub-circuits, several cable types and containment, terminated and working. It's split into sub-sections, and since 2021 you can re-sit a failed sub-section on its own. The V1.1 checklist lists: TP&N DB and protective devices; a 20A TP&N BS EN 60309 supply in XLPE SWA for an EV pillar; a 3-phase DOL motor circuit in SY with remote start/stop; lighting with 2-way, intermediate and key switches; a BS 1363 ring in singles; a CO detector safety circuit in FP200; Cat 5 data outlets; main bonding to gas and water; an S-plan heating/hot water system with a solar thermal element in heat-resistant flex; metal and PVC conduit.","items":["Can I read a full spec and drawing pack and pull out every dimension, height, cable type/size and device rating before I start fixing?","Can I bend steel and PVC conduit (90s, sets, bridge/saddle sets) to marked measurements, and cut, ream, thread and couple it neatly?","Can I install trunking and tray (or whatever containment the spec gives) level, secure and to the stated fixing centres?","Can I gland and terminate XLPE SWA properly (armour cut to length, gland fully tightened, banjo/earth tag, shroud) at both ends?","Can I dress, terminate and label a three-phase TP&N board with the correct MCB/RCBO/RCD types and ratings as specified, with line conductors on the right phases?","Can I wire a DOL starter for a 3-phase motor with remote start/stop (control circuit, holding contact, overload) and get the rotation right?","Can I wire two-way, intermediate and key-switch lighting in singles and T&E with correct switch-line identification (brown sleeving on switched conductors)?","Can I wire a ring final in singles in containment and keep both legs clearly identified at the board?","Can I install FP200-type cable with fire-rated clips/supports and terminate it correctly for a safety service (CO detector)?","Can I wire an S-plan system (programmer, room and cylinder stats, two 2-port valves, pump, boiler interlock, solar thermal controller) from a wiring centre diagram in heat-resistant flex?","Can I terminate Cat 5 data outlets to the correct standard (T568A/B, as specified) and keep ELV data properly segregated from LV?","Can I install main protective bonding to gas and water with the correct csa, BS 951 clamps and labels, in the right place relative to the meter/stopcock?","Is every termination tight, with the correct strip length (no bare copper showing, no insulation under the screw), and correct conductor colours and sleeving?","Can I plan my time across sub-sections so every circuit is finished and working, not just the parts I enjoy?","Do I leave the work area safe and tidy, with all covers and blanks fitted and nothing live that shouldn't be?"],"fails":["Not following the specification (wrong position or height, wrong cable type/size, wrong device), even when the work is safe","Wrong protective device type or rating, or undersized cable","Loose terminations or glands not fully tightened","Too much or too little insulation stripped; incorrect conductor identification or missing sleeving","Circuits that don't work when energised (motor control and S-plan are the classic ones)","Poor segregation of ELV data/safety circuits from LV","Running out of time and leaving a sub-section incomplete"]},{"id":"s3","title":"B - Inspection, testing and certification","time":"3.5 h (all NET sources); the manual adds about 20 min for preparation","what":"Inspect and test your own installation to GN3 methods, record results as you go, compare them against BS 7671 limits and complete an Electrical Installation Certificate with the schedule of circuit details and schedule of test results. Your 2391 background helps a lot here, but the assessor is marking method and paperwork as well as numbers.","items":["Can I do a methodical visual inspection and complete the inspection schedule honestly?","Can I check the test instrument (condition, leads, calibration in date), null the leads and select the right function and range for each test?","Can I test continuity of protective conductors, including main and any supplementary bonding, and record R1+R2 or R2 correctly?","Can I do the three-step ring final continuity test (end-to-end r1, rn, r2; cross-connect; check at every socket) and explain what the readings tell me?","Can I do insulation resistance at the correct test voltage, with sensitive equipment disconnected or accounted for and every switch and isolator operated so the whole circuit is tested?","Can I confirm polarity on every circuit, including switch lines and ES lampholder centre contacts where fitted?","Can I measure Ze and Zs safely and compare Zs against the BS 7671 tabulated maximums (using the 0.8 rule of thumb or corrected values)?","Can I measure and record PFC/PSCC for three-phase correctly (and know when the value needs doubling if the instrument measures phase to neutral only; check the current guidance)?","Can I check phase sequence with a rotation tester and confirm motor rotation?","Can I test RCD/RCBO operation (additional protection) at the required test current and record the trip time, and press the test button?","Can I functionally test every switch, isolator, interlock, motor control and heating control?","Can I fill in an EIC, schedule of circuit details and schedule of test results clearly and completely, with nothing left blank that should have an entry or N/A?","Do I record each result immediately, not from memory at the end?","Do I reconnect and re-tighten everything I disconnected for testing and refit all covers?"],"fails":["Instrument not checked, leads not nulled, or wrong range or test voltage","Not following GN3 methods or sequence","Results not recorded as the test was done","Not checking results against BS 7671 limits","Incomplete or inaccurate EIC or schedules","Not operating all switches or isolators during insulation resistance or continuity, so parts of the circuit go untested","Loose connections left after disconnecting for tests"]},{"id":"s4","title":"C - Safe isolation of circuits","time":"30 min (NET AM2E page and V1.1 checklist); the manual adds about 5 min for preparation","what":"Three separate isolations while other circuits stay live: single-phase equipment, three-phase equipment, and a whole distribution board (the one used for fault diagnosis). You must isolate only what is needed and prove it dead every time.","items":["Can I pick out and isolate a single piece of single-phase equipment without killing other circuits, and prove it dead at the point of work?","Can I isolate a three-phase load and prove all phases, neutral and earth combinations dead at the equipment?","Can I isolate a complete DB and do the full 10-point test?","Do I prove the tester before and after on every one of the three isolations, not just the first?","Do I lock off and fit notices on every isolation, keeping each key on me?","Can I tell a functional switch from a proper isolating device, and choose the right one?","Can I spot and deal with the possibility of a second supply, borrowed neutral or backfeed?"],"fails":["Getting lazy on the second or third isolation and skipping prove-test-prove","Isolating more than needed, or using a device that isn't suitable for isolation","Testing only L-N on three-phase equipment","No lock or notice on one of the three tasks"]},{"id":"s5","title":"D - Fault diagnosis and rectification","time":"2 h (all NET sources); the manual adds about 5 min for preparation","what":"You're given symptom information for faults on a rig. For each one, work out the fault type (open circuit, short circuit, high resistance or mis-connection), exactly where it is (between which two points and on which conductor), and how you'd fix it and prove the repair. How many faults you get isn't published - check with NET or your centre.","items":["Can I read symptom sheets and circuit diagrams and write a short plan before testing?","Can I safely isolate and prove dead before any dead testing (the safe-isolation rules apply here too)?","Can I choose the right instrument and range (low-ohm continuity vs insulation resistance) for each suspected fault type?","Can I narrow a fault down by splitting the circuit at accessible points and recording readings as I go?","Can I tell the four fault types apart from readings: open (infinite/very high continuity), short (near-zero IR between conductors), high resistance (a raised but finite reading), mis-connection (wrong polarity or crossed conductors)?","Can I find faults on lighting (2-way/intermediate), rings (including an interconnection or a broken leg), motor control circuits and heating controls?","Can I state the location precisely: 'between point X and point Y on the CPC', not 'somewhere in the lighting'?","Can I write down a sensible repair method and the tests I'd do afterwards to prove it?","Can I leave the rig as I found it: covers back, connections tight, isolation removed properly only when instructed?"],"fails":["Vague fault locations (not naming both points and the conductor)","Naming the wrong fault type (for example calling high resistance an open circuit)","Doing live work or dead testing without proper isolation","Guessing instead of testing, and running out of time","No verification tests stated after rectification"]},{"id":"s6","title":"E - Assessment of applied knowledge (online)","time":"Check with NET: the NET AM2E page says 1 h / 30 questions; the AM2E V1.1 checklist says 1.5 h / 40 questions (AM2S v1 is 1.5 h / 45 questions)","what":"A computer-based multiple-choice test on health and safety, BS 7671, Building Regulations (including Part P) and inspection, testing and fault finding. The provided publications are open-book, and no other documents are allowed.","items":["Can I find things quickly in BS 7671: Part 4 disconnection times, Appendix 3 max Zs tables, Appendix 4 cable ratings and correction factors, Part 7 special locations, Part 6 test values?","Can I find answers fast in the IET On-Site Guide (cable selection tables, standard final circuits, max lengths)?","Can I find things in GN3 (test methods, minimum IR values, test voltages, RCD test requirements)?","Do I know my way around the Electrician's Guide to the Building Regulations (Part P notifiable work, Parts B, E, F, L, M and how they affect electrical work)? Check the Welsh versus English Approved Document differences.","Can I answer core H&S law questions (HASAWA, EAWR 1989, CDM, RIDDOR, COSHH, working at height, safe isolation principles)?","Can I do quick calcs: Ib/In/Iz selection with correction factors, voltage drop, Zs from R1+R2 plus Ze, the 0.8 rule for Zs?","Have I done timed practice multiple-choice papers against the clock?","Do I know which BS 7671 edition and amendment the centre supplies? (FAQ says edition-neutral with A2 incorporated since 2022; check with NET whether A3/A4 is now used.)"],"fails":["Not being able to find the right table or regulation in BS 7671 fast enough","Weak Building Regulations knowledge (commonly flagged by NET)","Spending too long on one question and leaving others unanswered"]}],"tips":["Confirm which version you're on (AM2E if EWA registered before 03/09/2023, AM2E v1 from 04/09/2023). Timings, checklist and fee differ.","Before booking, get the AM2E checklist signed by you, your training provider and employer/nominated person (valid for 6 months). Have your EWQ certificate, learner history and skills scan front page ready.","Download and read the current NET Pre-Assessment Manual for your version from the NET site. Everything provided on the day (drawings, forms) follows it.","Your own tools: only insulated side cutters, strippers and pliers (BS EN IEC 60900 recommended), in good condition. The centre supplies everything else and you must use theirs.","Bring safety boots and suitable workwear. Check with your centre whether they provide other PPE (glasses, gloves).","Phones and smartwatches are collected at the start of each day. Plan for no clock on your wrist and use the centre clock.","Publications provided: BS 7671, GN3, On-Site Guide, Electrician's Guide to Building Regulations. Don't take the Candidate Manual out of the assessment area (disqualification).","NET gives a total of about 17 h 45 min, usually spread over about 2.5 days (check the exact schedule with your centre). Sleep well and eat properly; the install day is long.","Read the whole spec first and highlight every dimension, cable type and device rating. Most installation fails are 'didn't follow the spec', not unsafe work.","Treat safe isolation as automatic: prove, test, prove, lock, notice. Every time, in every section, including fault finding.","Record test results as you take them, and fill every box on the certificate or put N/A.","Practise conduit bending, the DOL starter and S-plan wiring before the day. These are the areas experienced domestic or commercial sparks most often haven't done recently.","There's no feedback on the day. Results come by email about 5 working days after marking, with a general statement if you fail.","Re-sits: at least 10 days later (NET suggests 3+ weeks with training), all re-sit sections in one booking on one date, and passed sections expire after 2 years. You can re-sit individual failed installation sub-sections.","If you need reasonable adjustments, apply in advance under NET's Special Considerations and Reasonable Adjustments policy."]};
